WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.019
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:13.870 --> 00:00:16.960
PRESENTER: Well, in case anyone
here doesn't happen to know,

00:00:16.960 --> 00:00:19.911
Cliff Shull is the 1994
laureate in physics.

00:00:19.911 --> 00:00:23.348
[APPLAUSE]

00:00:47.800 --> 00:00:51.670
Cliff is sharing the honor with
Bert Brockhouse from McMaster.

00:00:51.670 --> 00:00:54.400
And the Nobel citation,
in fact, quoted Cliff

00:00:54.400 --> 00:00:57.010
as being the one who told
us where the atoms are,

00:00:57.010 --> 00:00:59.800
and Brockhouse telling
us what they do.

00:00:59.800 --> 00:01:03.550
Cliff was born in
Pittsburgh in 1915.

00:01:03.550 --> 00:01:05.710
There is a-- perhaps
apocryphal-- story

00:01:05.710 --> 00:01:09.730
that his great skill and
intuition in the laboratory

00:01:09.730 --> 00:01:12.580
came from his family hardware
and home repair business

00:01:12.580 --> 00:01:15.900
in which he was quite active,
I understand, as a youth.

00:01:15.900 --> 00:01:19.540
Got his degree at
Carnegie Tech in 1937,

00:01:19.540 --> 00:01:22.600
and went off to NYU for
his graduate studies.

00:01:22.600 --> 00:01:25.510
There, he got into
the beginnings

00:01:25.510 --> 00:01:28.630
of what you might call
nuclear technology with an MIT

00:01:28.630 --> 00:01:30.640
connection, as during
his thesis time,

00:01:30.640 --> 00:01:33.040
he built a Van de
Graaff generator.

00:01:33.040 --> 00:01:37.480
And of course, Van de Graaff was
a professor here at that time.

00:01:37.480 --> 00:01:41.480
He did not, however, work
with neutrons at that time.

00:01:41.480 --> 00:01:45.340
And in fact, during
the war, as well,

00:01:45.340 --> 00:01:49.900
did not get to work with
developing neutron technology,

00:01:49.900 --> 00:01:53.140
as he was too valuable
in his work with Texaco--

00:01:53.140 --> 00:01:55.120
or what is now Texaco--

00:01:55.120 --> 00:01:59.770
working on high-octane
aviation fuel during the war.

00:01:59.770 --> 00:02:01.720
This time was productive,
however, of course,

00:02:01.720 --> 00:02:02.860
in a different way.

00:02:02.860 --> 00:02:09.100
In 1941, he married Martha, with
a very important partnership--

00:02:09.100 --> 00:02:14.230
50-plus years and going on.

00:02:14.230 --> 00:02:18.640
He did get to work with X-ray
diffraction techniques, which

00:02:18.640 --> 00:02:20.770
presumably played a
major part in seeding

00:02:20.770 --> 00:02:23.440
his subsequent
activities with neutrons.

00:02:23.440 --> 00:02:26.690
After the war, Cliff went
to Oak Ridge, and using

00:02:26.690 --> 00:02:28.570
the large graphite reactor--

00:02:28.570 --> 00:02:29.770
which was developed there--

00:02:29.770 --> 00:02:32.920
began his study of
neutron techniques,

00:02:32.920 --> 00:02:36.880
working on that for many
decades, and of course,

00:02:36.880 --> 00:02:40.600
resulting in the prize
which he deserves today.

00:02:40.600 --> 00:02:43.404
I will not get into
the work with neutrons.

00:02:43.404 --> 00:02:44.570
You'll hear that from Cliff.

00:02:44.570 --> 00:02:47.960
You'll hear some further
remarks from other people.

00:02:47.960 --> 00:02:52.700
In fact, I'll just end with some
departmental personal remarks.

00:02:52.700 --> 00:02:56.420
First of all, I think that
Cliff's colleagues have

00:02:56.420 --> 00:02:58.720
a deep appreciation to Cliff
for his many contributions

00:02:58.720 --> 00:03:03.160
to the department,
his pleasant aspect,

00:03:03.160 --> 00:03:10.240
his dedication to high standards
and to re-doing experiments.

00:03:10.240 --> 00:03:13.330
And everyone is
extremely delighted

00:03:13.330 --> 00:03:15.520
with this recognition.

00:03:15.520 --> 00:03:17.530
Indeed, Cliff won
The Buckley Prize--

00:03:17.530 --> 00:03:20.320
a very prestigious prize in
condensed matter physics--

00:03:20.320 --> 00:03:25.720
in the mid-50s, and this is
a 0-year overdue recognition

00:03:25.720 --> 00:03:29.230
by the Nobel Committee.

00:03:29.230 --> 00:03:34.010
Cliff, with his
characteristic modesty,

00:03:34.010 --> 00:03:37.630
I think is genuinely
surprised by the news he

00:03:37.630 --> 00:03:39.071
received a few days ago.

00:03:39.071 --> 00:03:40.570
And I would just
say that many of us

00:03:40.570 --> 00:03:43.780
were far less surprised than
he was, since we all feel very,

00:03:43.780 --> 00:03:46.000
very strongly this
was a very timely

00:03:46.000 --> 00:03:48.130
and a very, very
well-deserved prize.

00:03:48.130 --> 00:03:51.610
So we're going to have a few
remarks now before Cliff gets

00:03:51.610 --> 00:03:53.770
his chance to tell
you about neutrons

00:03:53.770 --> 00:03:56.380
and how he helped develop
this important field.

00:03:56.380 --> 00:03:59.890
Chuck Vest, the President,
will say a few things.

00:03:59.890 --> 00:04:03.040
The Dean, Bob Birgeneau,
who is one of our most

00:04:03.040 --> 00:04:04.960
active, certainly,
neutron-scatterers today

00:04:04.960 --> 00:04:05.960
will add something.

00:04:05.960 --> 00:04:08.770
And then finally, one of
Cliff's students, Dave Moncton,

00:04:08.770 --> 00:04:11.900
who has flown in from Argonne,
will give a brief perspective

00:04:11.900 --> 00:04:13.705
from the students' view.

00:04:13.705 --> 00:04:14.230
So, Chuck.

00:04:18.140 --> 00:04:20.990
VEST: Thank you
very much, Ernie.

00:04:20.990 --> 00:04:25.340
I think that moments
like this are extremely

00:04:25.340 --> 00:04:29.930
important in the life of a
great institution like MIT,

00:04:29.930 --> 00:04:32.540
and certainly are extremely
important in the life

00:04:32.540 --> 00:04:34.730
of science period.

00:04:34.730 --> 00:04:37.250
It's a great honor
to be associated

00:04:37.250 --> 00:04:41.090
with people who stand
for such excellence

00:04:41.090 --> 00:04:46.370
in their scientific work and
in their being as humans.

00:04:46.370 --> 00:04:49.190
There are two things that
particularly struck me

00:04:49.190 --> 00:04:51.750
as I listened and
talked to people--

00:04:51.750 --> 00:04:53.870
I must admit, from a
distance, because I've been

00:04:53.870 --> 00:04:56.380
out of town the last two days--

00:04:56.380 --> 00:04:58.850
and had to sense all this
through the newspapers

00:04:58.850 --> 00:05:00.230
and the television.

00:05:00.230 --> 00:05:03.080
And running into a couple
of people, including

00:05:03.080 --> 00:05:05.990
a former physics student
here from the Department,

00:05:05.990 --> 00:05:07.610
in the Washington area.

00:05:07.610 --> 00:05:11.060
The two things that struck
me the most were first--

00:05:11.060 --> 00:05:15.620
every single person I have
talked to about this award,

00:05:15.620 --> 00:05:18.260
within the first one
or two sentences,

00:05:18.260 --> 00:05:21.020
has used the word "nice."

00:05:21.020 --> 00:05:24.110
Professor Shull is known
as a nice person, which

00:05:24.110 --> 00:05:27.830
is another way of saying a warm
human being and somebody who

00:05:27.830 --> 00:05:31.370
is viewed as being very
supportive of his students

00:05:31.370 --> 00:05:34.850
and his colleagues, and to
the Institute community.

00:05:34.850 --> 00:05:36.800
The second thing
that really struck me

00:05:36.800 --> 00:05:38.750
was a quote in the newspaper.

00:05:38.750 --> 00:05:40.550
It may have been
from Bob Birgeneau.

00:05:40.550 --> 00:05:43.880
I'm sorry if I'm attributing
it to the wrong person--

00:05:43.880 --> 00:05:49.340
that said that they had
worried that the revolution

00:05:49.340 --> 00:05:52.280
that Professor Shull
and his colleagues

00:05:52.280 --> 00:05:56.120
brought about in the use of
neutron scattering to measure

00:05:56.120 --> 00:05:58.550
properties of
materials and so forth

00:05:58.550 --> 00:06:01.490
had become so standard,
so well-known, so much

00:06:01.490 --> 00:06:04.370
the stuff of textbooks
that they were afraid

00:06:04.370 --> 00:06:08.960
that the person who originated
it might be forgotten.

00:06:08.960 --> 00:06:11.930
That is a very strong and
very important statement,

00:06:11.930 --> 00:06:15.740
and I think that one of the
terrific benefits of the Nobel

00:06:15.740 --> 00:06:18.990
Prize recognition is
that it reminds people--

00:06:18.990 --> 00:06:21.050
and particularly young people--

00:06:21.050 --> 00:06:23.690
that there are people
behind science,

00:06:23.690 --> 00:06:27.680
and it is their passion, their
enthusiasm, their commitment

00:06:27.680 --> 00:06:30.590
to excellence and belief in
the importance of science

00:06:30.590 --> 00:06:32.390
that really drives it forward.

00:06:32.390 --> 00:06:37.310
So we thank you very much-- to
use a rather hackneyed phrase,

00:06:37.310 --> 00:06:37.940
I suppose--

00:06:37.940 --> 00:06:42.170
for being such a fantastic
role model for those who follow

00:06:42.170 --> 00:06:43.970
behind you, Professor Shull.

00:06:43.970 --> 00:06:45.680
And it's just a very,
very great honor

00:06:45.680 --> 00:06:48.320
to be a member of
this MIT community

00:06:48.320 --> 00:06:50.300
and to share this
moment with you.

00:06:50.300 --> 00:06:51.695
Thank you very much.

00:06:51.695 --> 00:06:53.175
[APPLAUSE]

00:06:53.175 --> 00:06:53.675
Bob.

00:06:59.504 --> 00:07:01.670
BIRGENEAU: As many of my
friends out there are know,

00:07:01.670 --> 00:07:08.540
this prize is a really
extraordinary pleasure for me,

00:07:08.540 --> 00:07:11.630
because actually, I began my
research career as a summer

00:07:11.630 --> 00:07:14.630
student in Bert Brockhouse's
group, Chalk River.

00:07:14.630 --> 00:07:16.640
And it was because
of Bert Brockhouse--

00:07:16.640 --> 00:07:19.320
who also is a nice,
wonderful person-- so this

00:07:19.320 --> 00:07:23.630
is extraordinary that we had
two such special human beings

00:07:23.630 --> 00:07:26.960
recognized with one prize.

00:07:26.960 --> 00:07:28.820
And in fact, my
first publication

00:07:28.820 --> 00:07:31.324
was with Brockhouse's group,
and embarrassingly, it's still

00:07:31.324 --> 00:07:32.615
my most referenced publication.

00:07:32.615 --> 00:07:35.060
[LAUGHTER]

00:07:35.060 --> 00:07:37.610
After having got
launched by Brockhouse,

00:07:37.610 --> 00:07:40.870
I then meandered around
the world, actually,

00:07:40.870 --> 00:07:43.070
and then finally
ended up here at MIT.

00:07:43.070 --> 00:07:47.390
And while I was learning about
this field from Brockhouse

00:07:47.390 --> 00:07:50.840
and Brockhouse's
former cohorts, they

00:07:50.840 --> 00:07:52.970
had told me how there
was this genius who

00:07:52.970 --> 00:07:54.680
had created the field
in the first place

00:07:54.680 --> 00:07:57.890
by the name of Cliff Shull, who
was then at MIT, so it was one

00:07:57.890 --> 00:08:00.290
of my goals to come
to MIT and to meet

00:08:00.290 --> 00:08:01.701
the great person himself.

00:08:01.701 --> 00:08:04.200
And I remember first walking
down the hallway the first time

00:08:04.200 --> 00:08:06.860
I saw Cliff, and Cliff was
so nice and so self-effacing,

00:08:06.860 --> 00:08:07.730
I couldn't believe--

00:08:07.730 --> 00:08:09.359
especially in the
MIT environment--

00:08:09.359 --> 00:08:10.400
that this modest person--

00:08:10.400 --> 00:08:11.340
[LAUGHTER]

00:08:11.340 --> 00:08:13.990
--was this great hero
that I had heard about.

00:08:13.990 --> 00:08:15.420
But indeed, it was.

00:08:15.420 --> 00:08:19.670
And actually, we had
many years together.

00:08:19.670 --> 00:08:21.590
We served on a number
of academy committees

00:08:21.590 --> 00:08:24.620
together, flying back
and forth to Washington.

00:08:24.620 --> 00:08:28.270
And we shared one extreme
vice, which is both Cliff

00:08:28.270 --> 00:08:30.050
and I like to smoke cigars.

00:08:30.050 --> 00:08:34.850
And Cliff had the wisdom to
retire before it was banned.

00:08:34.850 --> 00:08:36.440
But I presume--
actually, there's

00:08:36.440 --> 00:08:37.970
still a residue in
his office if you

00:08:37.970 --> 00:08:40.400
go into his office,
which is now occupied

00:08:40.400 --> 00:08:42.200
by my and Marc
Kastner's post-docs, who

00:08:42.200 --> 00:08:45.920
are hoping that some of this
greatness rubs off on them.

00:08:45.920 --> 00:08:47.990
Besides being a
wonderful colleague

00:08:47.990 --> 00:08:51.410
and an outstanding
scientist, Cliff also

00:08:51.410 --> 00:08:56.750
was excellent as a undergraduate
and graduate student teacher,

00:08:56.750 --> 00:08:58.400
and as a graduate mentor.

00:08:58.400 --> 00:09:01.670
And he educated many of
the leaders in the field.

00:09:01.670 --> 00:09:05.570
One of the premier of
those is Dave Moncton,

00:09:05.570 --> 00:09:08.810
who's Associate Director at
Argonne National Laboratory,

00:09:08.810 --> 00:09:11.900
and who is Head of the
Advanced Photon Source, which

00:09:11.900 --> 00:09:16.100
is this wonderful new source
which we and many others hope

00:09:16.100 --> 00:09:21.220
are going to advance the kind
of science that Cliff has done--

00:09:21.220 --> 00:09:25.160
albeit using protons instead
of neutrons, in that case,

00:09:25.160 --> 00:09:26.010
a level further.

00:09:26.010 --> 00:09:27.770
So maybe Dave can
make a few comments

00:09:27.770 --> 00:09:29.716
from the perspective
of someone who

00:09:29.716 --> 00:09:31.340
used to sit here as
a graduate student.

00:09:34.280 --> 00:09:37.710
[APPLAUSE]

00:09:40.650 --> 00:09:43.400
MONCTON: I'm really
overwhelmed by the privilege

00:09:43.400 --> 00:09:47.240
of being able to stand up
here, have the opportunity

00:09:47.240 --> 00:09:50.090
to thank Cliff
personally in front

00:09:50.090 --> 00:09:53.480
of this particular
audience for all

00:09:53.480 --> 00:09:56.825
that he gave to me as a student.

00:09:56.825 --> 00:09:59.200
I'd like to speak mostly to
the students in the audience.

00:09:59.200 --> 00:10:03.070
I guess the faculty members
can listen in if they'd like.

00:10:03.070 --> 00:10:07.510
The first contact
that I had with Cliff

00:10:07.510 --> 00:10:10.500
was when I was still
a senior at Cornell.

00:10:10.500 --> 00:10:14.320
I had applied to only one
school as an undergraduate,

00:10:14.320 --> 00:10:18.430
and realized that that might
be risky as a graduate student.

00:10:18.430 --> 00:10:20.140
So I had applied to
a number of places,

00:10:20.140 --> 00:10:23.130
and I was fortunate to have
gotten accepted at all of them.

00:10:23.130 --> 00:10:25.600
And I was fortunate, also,
to have gotten scholarships

00:10:25.600 --> 00:10:26.830
at all of them.

00:10:26.830 --> 00:10:28.810
So I was having a hard
time making up my mind

00:10:28.810 --> 00:10:30.310
until a letter
arrived in the mail,

00:10:30.310 --> 00:10:33.020
and it was a personal
letter from Cliff,

00:10:33.020 --> 00:10:35.640
who had been busy in the
graduate school office looking

00:10:35.640 --> 00:10:39.000
over the applications
of graduate students

00:10:39.000 --> 00:10:41.790
and personally writing to
those people he thought

00:10:41.790 --> 00:10:44.730
would fit into his laboratory.

00:10:44.730 --> 00:10:47.520
And there was no
question in my mind,

00:10:47.520 --> 00:10:49.260
when I read that
letter, that this

00:10:49.260 --> 00:10:50.610
was where I wanted to work.

00:10:50.610 --> 00:10:53.160
I don't think I took
my cap and gown off

00:10:53.160 --> 00:10:56.340
before I got in the car and
drove from Cornell to Cambridge

00:10:56.340 --> 00:11:00.390
and began working in
the summer of 1970.

00:11:00.390 --> 00:11:02.750
I think the 1st of
July, essentially.

00:11:02.750 --> 00:11:07.920
I was so excited about a real
laboratory and real experiments

00:11:07.920 --> 00:11:10.350
that I couldn't think
of anything better

00:11:10.350 --> 00:11:12.450
to do that summer.

00:11:12.450 --> 00:11:17.980
That's an example of Cliff's
fastidiousness, I suppose,

00:11:17.980 --> 00:11:20.070
that has become legendary.

00:11:20.070 --> 00:11:23.310
And all of his students
will tell you about that,

00:11:23.310 --> 00:11:26.970
and it's a gift that I think
he's given all of his students.

00:11:26.970 --> 00:11:30.360
The other night,
my wife and I were

00:11:30.360 --> 00:11:32.530
arguing why it was that
when I go to sleep at night,

00:11:32.530 --> 00:11:34.738
I like to have all the
three-way switches in my house

00:11:34.738 --> 00:11:36.930
all in the down position.

00:11:36.930 --> 00:11:39.870
You don't need to have them
all in the down position.

00:11:39.870 --> 00:11:41.916
As long as they're
up in pairs, then

00:11:41.916 --> 00:11:43.290
the lights will
all still be off.

00:11:43.290 --> 00:11:44.640
[LAUGHTER]

00:11:44.640 --> 00:11:50.644
But it seemed to me that that
was not fastidious enough.

00:11:50.644 --> 00:11:52.185
I mean, after all,
if someone came in

00:11:52.185 --> 00:11:53.520
in the middle of the night
and turned on a light

00:11:53.520 --> 00:11:54.870
and turned it off,
I mean, you'd know

00:11:54.870 --> 00:11:56.536
if you remembered
where you'd left them.

00:11:56.536 --> 00:11:59.760
[LAUGHTER]

00:11:59.760 --> 00:12:05.016
So throughout my career
as an experimentalist,

00:12:05.016 --> 00:12:06.590
I always tried to emulate.

00:12:06.590 --> 00:12:09.930
I never successfully emulated
that level of fastidiousness

00:12:09.930 --> 00:12:12.960
that Cliff brought to bear
on experimental science,

00:12:12.960 --> 00:12:14.850
but I never regret it.

00:12:14.850 --> 00:12:17.640
Any time I did something,
I lined a goniometer up

00:12:17.640 --> 00:12:21.540
to be a 0 of angle when it was
perfectly vertical or perfectly

00:12:21.540 --> 00:12:22.080
horizontal.

00:12:22.080 --> 00:12:25.050
It didn't have to be
defined as 0 there,

00:12:25.050 --> 00:12:28.530
but that just gave you some clue
as to whether it had been moved

00:12:28.530 --> 00:12:30.390
inadvertently, or the
motors had slipped

00:12:30.390 --> 00:12:32.306
or what have you, because
you could line it up

00:12:32.306 --> 00:12:34.560
with various other vertical
clues in the horizon.

00:12:34.560 --> 00:12:36.150
And those were the
kinds of things

00:12:36.150 --> 00:12:40.280
that Cliff just did routinely.

00:12:40.280 --> 00:12:42.500
During my-- I think
early in my second year,

00:12:42.500 --> 00:12:45.650
Cliff thought it would be a good
exercise for a student to write

00:12:45.650 --> 00:12:47.870
a Nobel Prize recommendation.

00:12:47.870 --> 00:12:50.840
So he's told me that he would
like to see Brian Josephson get

00:12:50.840 --> 00:12:54.110
the Nobel Prize, and would
I go off to the library

00:12:54.110 --> 00:12:57.560
and take a crack at writing
the case for Brian Josephson?

00:12:57.560 --> 00:13:00.740
Which I was very enthusiastic
about, and I did that,

00:13:00.740 --> 00:13:02.600
and I gave it to Cliff.

00:13:02.600 --> 00:13:04.370
And he puckered his
lips as he usually

00:13:04.370 --> 00:13:07.655
did and held his pipe
out and said thank you.

00:13:07.655 --> 00:13:10.100
Of course, that was
the last I saw of it

00:13:10.100 --> 00:13:12.980
until Brian Josephson,
a few months later,

00:13:12.980 --> 00:13:15.109
got the Nobel Prize.

00:13:15.109 --> 00:13:16.400
And I thought, "That was easy!"

00:13:16.400 --> 00:13:19.760
[LAUGHTER]

00:13:23.610 --> 00:13:28.739
Little did I know what work goes
on in getting Nobel prizes--

00:13:28.739 --> 00:13:30.030
on either side of the equation.

00:13:33.790 --> 00:13:36.520
The one other gift--

00:13:36.520 --> 00:13:38.440
and the most important
gift that I think Cliff

00:13:38.440 --> 00:13:40.690
gave me as a teacher--

00:13:40.690 --> 00:13:45.430
was I guess what I call the
gift of self-confidence.

00:13:45.430 --> 00:13:47.620
As an experimental
physicist, it's

00:13:47.620 --> 00:13:49.870
very important-- there
are lots of bleak moments.

00:13:49.870 --> 00:13:53.850
There are long periods
between good results,

00:13:53.850 --> 00:13:57.460
and even longer periods
between excellent results,

00:13:57.460 --> 00:13:59.920
and longer between
publishable results,

00:13:59.920 --> 00:14:02.952
and longer even between
results that people

00:14:02.952 --> 00:14:04.660
want to read about
when you publish them.

00:14:04.660 --> 00:14:06.700
[LAUGHTER]

00:14:06.700 --> 00:14:09.245
And in those long
waits, you have

00:14:09.245 --> 00:14:10.870
to be confident that
you have something

00:14:10.870 --> 00:14:13.780
to bring to bear on the field--

00:14:13.780 --> 00:14:14.590
something to offer.

00:14:14.590 --> 00:14:17.260
And you need to have a lot
of confidence in yourself.

00:14:17.260 --> 00:14:20.380
And what Cliff did
was, to a large extent,

00:14:20.380 --> 00:14:25.000
he left me alone, because
he had what I believe--

00:14:25.000 --> 00:14:28.990
he can correct me when
he gets his chance--

00:14:28.990 --> 00:14:32.306
what I believe is a lot
of confidence in me.

00:14:32.306 --> 00:14:34.180
I didn't work on a
problem that Cliff gave me

00:14:34.180 --> 00:14:37.107
as a thesis advisor, and
that was very unusual.

00:14:37.107 --> 00:14:38.690
And I didn't even
work in his own lab,

00:14:38.690 --> 00:14:39.773
finally, on my own thesis.

00:14:39.773 --> 00:14:42.870
I went to Brookhaven and
worked at their reactor.

00:14:42.870 --> 00:14:47.560
And throughout the entire period
of my graduate school, he was--

00:14:47.560 --> 00:14:49.330
especially in the
later years-- almost

00:14:49.330 --> 00:14:52.440
a secondary figure in
my day-to-day life.

00:14:52.440 --> 00:14:56.680
But this tremendous confidence
that he had in me translated--

00:14:56.680 --> 00:14:59.020
some magical fashion--

00:14:59.020 --> 00:15:02.770
into my own self-confidence
as an experimentalist.

00:15:02.770 --> 00:15:05.174
So it's not the--

00:15:05.174 --> 00:15:07.570
I don't know-- the book
learning, in a sense,

00:15:07.570 --> 00:15:12.400
that I took away from my
graduate education with Cliff.

00:15:12.400 --> 00:15:14.500
I took it away from MIT,
because goodness knows

00:15:14.500 --> 00:15:16.210
we had many, many courses.

00:15:16.210 --> 00:15:19.540
But what Cliff gave me-- and
I think all of his students--

00:15:19.540 --> 00:15:23.980
was that self-confidence that
he himself-- and you probably

00:15:23.980 --> 00:15:27.580
won't get him to admit it, but
he has it in great quantities.

00:15:27.580 --> 00:15:29.890
Quantities enough that
he is able to share it

00:15:29.890 --> 00:15:31.040
with so many of us.

00:15:31.040 --> 00:15:32.368
So thank you, Cliff.

00:15:32.368 --> 00:15:35.854
[APPLAUSE]

00:15:39.838 --> 00:15:41.394
PRESENTER: OK,
without further delay,

00:15:41.394 --> 00:15:43.060
Cliff will tell us
about the development

00:15:43.060 --> 00:15:44.272
of neutron scattering.

00:15:50.668 --> 00:15:52.144
SHULL: Thank you, gentlemen.

00:15:57.080 --> 00:15:59.420
As Ernie has mentioned--

00:15:59.420 --> 00:16:05.820
and Bob-- the field
of research that I

00:16:05.820 --> 00:16:12.330
want to mention this afternoon
goes back a long ways; back

00:16:12.330 --> 00:16:16.680
to the immediate years
following World War

00:16:16.680 --> 00:16:21.240
II, back in the late 1940s.

00:16:21.240 --> 00:16:25.770
And this research deals
with the development

00:16:25.770 --> 00:16:33.780
of techniques that can utilize
slow neutron radiation.

00:16:33.780 --> 00:16:43.150
Now, neutrons were
first discovered in 1932

00:16:43.150 --> 00:16:45.910
by Chadwick in England.

00:16:45.910 --> 00:16:52.000
And Chadwick found that if you
take an alpha particle source--

00:16:52.000 --> 00:16:55.540
and essentially, the only
alpha particle sources

00:16:55.540 --> 00:17:02.080
known at that time were from
naturally-occurring radium

00:17:02.080 --> 00:17:03.620
sources.

00:17:03.620 --> 00:17:10.740
If you surround a radium
sample with some material--

00:17:10.740 --> 00:17:13.859
most usually beryllium--

00:17:13.859 --> 00:17:17.079
you find that, emanating
from the beryllium,

00:17:17.079 --> 00:17:19.619
is an intense radiation alpha.

00:17:19.619 --> 00:17:22.992
And that was later
investigated by Rutherford

00:17:22.992 --> 00:17:24.450
and other people--

00:17:24.450 --> 00:17:29.580
classic people-- and
labeled alpha particles.

00:17:29.580 --> 00:17:38.900
Now, it was also found, shortly
after Chadwick discovered--

00:17:38.900 --> 00:17:43.610
when Chadwick was arranging
such configurations--

00:17:43.610 --> 00:17:48.710
alpha particles sources--
he found that if you further

00:17:48.710 --> 00:17:55.100
surrounded that source
with other media,

00:17:55.100 --> 00:17:58.040
that there seemed to be
something coming away

00:17:58.040 --> 00:18:03.590
from the other media which
was certainly different

00:18:03.590 --> 00:18:07.580
than the alpha particles
that were originally released

00:18:07.580 --> 00:18:11.090
from the inner piece of radium.

00:18:11.090 --> 00:18:13.770
And on investigating
this, he found

00:18:13.770 --> 00:18:16.160
that it was a very
penetrating radiation,

00:18:16.160 --> 00:18:18.710
and that was the
surprising thing to him.

00:18:18.710 --> 00:18:24.860
It's radiation that could
pass through copious thickness

00:18:24.860 --> 00:18:31.119
of the material, and there was
no known radiation at the time

00:18:31.119 --> 00:18:31.910
that would do that.

00:18:35.080 --> 00:18:40.950
So with further studies
of the production

00:18:40.950 --> 00:18:43.980
of this anomalous radiation
with different materials

00:18:43.980 --> 00:18:50.520
surrounding the alpha
particle source,

00:18:50.520 --> 00:18:54.330
he came to think of this as
being some neutral particle

00:18:54.330 --> 00:18:56.220
type of radiation.

00:18:56.220 --> 00:18:59.550
The reason being that
a neutral particle

00:18:59.550 --> 00:19:02.550
would be expected to have
much greater penetration

00:19:02.550 --> 00:19:07.770
through media than any sort
of charged particle radiation.

00:19:07.770 --> 00:19:11.190
It was ruled out that it
was an electromagnetic type

00:19:11.190 --> 00:19:15.190
of radiation from
certain observations.

00:19:15.190 --> 00:19:17.550
So if it had no charge--

00:19:17.550 --> 00:19:22.230
if it were radiation
without charge--

00:19:22.230 --> 00:19:26.310
you could think of
it as being somehow

00:19:26.310 --> 00:19:29.640
neutral in an electrical charge.

00:19:29.640 --> 00:19:33.640
And he gave it
the name neutron--

00:19:33.640 --> 00:19:36.580
the neutron radiation.

00:19:36.580 --> 00:19:40.720
Well, this was very exciting
to nuclear physics--

00:19:40.720 --> 00:19:43.950
what there was of nuclear
physics at the time.

00:19:43.950 --> 00:19:48.030
Nuclear physics was
very rudimentary then.

00:19:48.030 --> 00:19:53.160
And further studies--
mostly by Fermi in Italy--

00:19:53.160 --> 00:19:59.430
showed that this radiation could
be effectively thermalized by,

00:19:59.430 --> 00:20:02.640
again, passing it
through and letting it

00:20:02.640 --> 00:20:06.340
bounce around in
some other medium.

00:20:06.340 --> 00:20:08.250
And if you observe
what comes out

00:20:08.250 --> 00:20:15.480
of this third piece of medium,
he obtained what he thought--

00:20:15.480 --> 00:20:20.040
what he classified-- as
thermal neutron radiation.

00:20:20.040 --> 00:20:22.260
How could he
determine that it had

00:20:22.260 --> 00:20:24.590
this sort of characteristic?

00:20:24.590 --> 00:20:28.560
By being thermalized, it
implied that the energy was

00:20:28.560 --> 00:20:32.400
down to thermal energy levels--

00:20:32.400 --> 00:20:36.090
thermal energy levels
that characterize

00:20:36.090 --> 00:20:42.550
atom emission of radiation--
thermal energy radiation.

00:20:42.550 --> 00:20:46.170
Well, one thing-- it was known
that captured cross-sections--

00:20:46.170 --> 00:20:50.130
absorption cross-sections
of materials--

00:20:50.130 --> 00:20:56.610
would be expected to follow
a certain energy dependence.

00:20:56.610 --> 00:21:01.260
The lower the energy, the
greater the absorption.

00:21:01.260 --> 00:21:04.720
And that, indeed,
was what he found--

00:21:04.720 --> 00:21:08.710
that if he put more
and more media there,

00:21:08.710 --> 00:21:14.160
did more and more acting
upon this neutron radiation,

00:21:14.160 --> 00:21:17.700
it lowered the energy
of the radiation

00:21:17.700 --> 00:21:21.930
so that you could observe a
larger absorption cross-section

00:21:21.930 --> 00:21:25.040
in something else.

00:21:25.040 --> 00:21:31.480
So this process could
continue until he obtained

00:21:31.480 --> 00:21:37.030
what he thought was real thermal
energy neutron radiation, where

00:21:37.030 --> 00:21:40.990
the neutrons had been degraded
in kinetic energy down

00:21:40.990 --> 00:21:46.800
to a thermal energy level of
some fraction of one electron

00:21:46.800 --> 00:21:48.340
volt--

00:21:48.340 --> 00:21:52.100
some fraction of
one electron volt.

00:21:52.100 --> 00:21:58.150
So this thermalization concept
was a very important one.

00:21:58.150 --> 00:22:00.850
It was shortly
recognized that if you

00:22:00.850 --> 00:22:06.170
had radiation of
such a low energy,

00:22:06.170 --> 00:22:14.480
that this radiation would also
exhibit a very anomalous wave

00:22:14.480 --> 00:22:16.880
character.

00:22:16.880 --> 00:22:22.160
And this goes back to the
heart of quantum mechanics.

00:22:22.160 --> 00:22:27.080
A particle moving along
is to be considered also

00:22:27.080 --> 00:22:29.930
as a moving wave.

00:22:29.930 --> 00:22:34.160
And there's a periodicity
associated with this wave.

00:22:34.160 --> 00:22:40.370
This first was suggested in
earlier years, most notably

00:22:40.370 --> 00:22:42.680
by de Broglie--

00:22:42.680 --> 00:22:47.780
de Broglie-- who suggested
that there was a wave length

00:22:47.780 --> 00:22:51.680
associated with moving
electron radiation.

00:22:51.680 --> 00:22:57.410
Moving electrons should also
exhibit a wave character.

00:22:57.410 --> 00:22:59.930
And it wasn't long
before people did

00:22:59.930 --> 00:23:04.130
experiments that
demonstrated sure, indeed,

00:23:04.130 --> 00:23:11.480
a moving electron does exhibit
a periodicity and a wave length,

00:23:11.480 --> 00:23:16.820
and that was demonstrated by
diffracting low-energy electron

00:23:16.820 --> 00:23:23.210
beams from crystals, just
as people with X-rays

00:23:23.210 --> 00:23:27.690
had demonstrated
many years earlier.

00:23:27.690 --> 00:23:30.500
So there was this weight
periodicity associated

00:23:30.500 --> 00:23:35.910
with moving electron particles.

00:23:35.910 --> 00:23:40.100
Now we have neutron particles
that are moving slowly,

00:23:40.100 --> 00:23:43.470
and they should have a
characteristic periodicity

00:23:43.470 --> 00:23:45.020
also.

00:23:45.020 --> 00:23:48.530
The question comes,
do these neutrons also

00:23:48.530 --> 00:23:51.830
show diffractive
effects, the same

00:23:51.830 --> 00:23:57.120
as had been observed with
electrons some years earlier?

00:23:57.120 --> 00:24:01.010
So there were two experiments--
two experimental groups--

00:24:01.010 --> 00:24:02.960
that set out to establish--

00:24:02.960 --> 00:24:08.900
to look for this periodicity;
this de Broglie wave character

00:24:08.900 --> 00:24:13.010
for this neutron radiation.

00:24:13.010 --> 00:24:19.480
Von Halban and Preiswerk in
France, and Mitchell and Powers

00:24:19.480 --> 00:24:23.840
at Columbia University
in the United States.

00:24:23.840 --> 00:24:29.030
Both pursuing
experimentation in 1936.

00:24:29.030 --> 00:24:32.870
And they both found, indeed,
that this periodicity

00:24:32.870 --> 00:24:34.010
was there--

00:24:34.010 --> 00:24:37.010
that there were
diffractive effects--

00:24:37.010 --> 00:24:37.520
excuse me.

00:24:41.250 --> 00:24:47.160
--from crystalline material
when this neutron radiation--

00:24:47.160 --> 00:24:52.830
thermal neutron radiation-- was
applied to these diffracting

00:24:52.830 --> 00:24:54.910
objects.

00:24:54.910 --> 00:24:57.540
So now we have a wavelength.

00:24:57.540 --> 00:24:58.990
We have a kinetic energy.

00:24:58.990 --> 00:25:00.360
We have an energy--

00:25:00.360 --> 00:25:02.880
a kinetic energy--
to be associated

00:25:02.880 --> 00:25:05.600
with these particles.

00:25:05.600 --> 00:25:08.390
Well, the experiments
of demonstrating

00:25:08.390 --> 00:25:12.420
this diffractive effect
were so complicated,

00:25:12.420 --> 00:25:15.080
and the intensities
that were involved

00:25:15.080 --> 00:25:20.810
were so small that one could
hardly think of exploiting it

00:25:20.810 --> 00:25:23.600
as a diffractive tool.

00:25:23.600 --> 00:25:27.500
X-ray diffraction was in a
well-defined, well-developed

00:25:27.500 --> 00:25:31.280
state by that time,
and many people

00:25:31.280 --> 00:25:34.220
were diffracting
X-rays and learning

00:25:34.220 --> 00:25:39.920
lots of things about crystals
and materials in general.

00:25:39.920 --> 00:25:44.780
One could think that
maybe neutrons could also

00:25:44.780 --> 00:25:49.040
serve that purpose, but the
intensities were frighteningly

00:25:49.040 --> 00:25:55.940
low, and nobody worried about
doing anything seriously

00:25:55.940 --> 00:25:58.160
in that direction.

00:25:58.160 --> 00:26:04.910
That all changed a couple
years later in 1939,

00:26:04.910 --> 00:26:11.750
when nuclear fission was
uncovered by Hahn and Meitner

00:26:11.750 --> 00:26:13.970
in Germany--

00:26:13.970 --> 00:26:16.380
1939.

00:26:16.380 --> 00:26:22.380
Now, the exciting thing about
nuclear fission is this.

00:26:22.380 --> 00:26:27.120
You bring neutrons onto
certain fissile nuclei--

00:26:27.120 --> 00:26:33.070
most notably uranium;
normal uranium--

00:26:33.070 --> 00:26:37.740
and you find that the
neutron absorbs the neutron

00:26:37.740 --> 00:26:43.140
and breaks up into fragments,
and more than two neutrons

00:26:43.140 --> 00:26:45.580
come off from that.

00:26:45.580 --> 00:26:49.540
One neutron in, more
than two coming off.

00:26:49.540 --> 00:26:53.320
Now, this conclusion
didn't come automatically.

00:26:53.320 --> 00:26:58.180
It came with a series of
different types of experiments.

00:26:58.180 --> 00:27:00.680
But that was very,
very interesting.

00:27:00.680 --> 00:27:07.280
I put in something, and I get
over twice that same thing out.

00:27:07.280 --> 00:27:11.240
That immediately suggests
that this process could

00:27:11.240 --> 00:27:18.110
be used in a cascade manner to
produce more than you feed in,

00:27:18.110 --> 00:27:24.320
and the process could
skyrocket, cascade, and become

00:27:24.320 --> 00:27:27.160
cataclysmic--

00:27:27.160 --> 00:27:31.470
with more coming out
than you're putting in.

00:27:31.470 --> 00:27:39.000
Well, it wasn't long after that
that we became engaged in war

00:27:39.000 --> 00:27:43.560
years, and it was proposed--

00:27:43.560 --> 00:27:46.110
thought very
seriously by people--

00:27:46.110 --> 00:27:49.330
that maybe this could be
developed into some sort

00:27:49.330 --> 00:27:49.830
of weapon--

00:27:52.650 --> 00:27:55.760
an atomic weapon.

00:27:55.760 --> 00:28:03.230
Maybe you could have a
configurational system that

00:28:03.230 --> 00:28:05.100
could be triggered
somehow or other,

00:28:05.100 --> 00:28:07.135
and it would just diverge.

00:28:09.710 --> 00:28:14.240
So many people became
involved in that--

00:28:14.240 --> 00:28:18.020
Fermi himself, and
many of his colleagues,

00:28:18.020 --> 00:28:20.600
and very well-known
people became

00:28:20.600 --> 00:28:23.210
involved in that
through what was

00:28:23.210 --> 00:28:28.790
called the Manhattan Project,
set up in the early war years.

00:28:32.520 --> 00:28:37.410
It was finally
demonstrated, in experiments

00:28:37.410 --> 00:28:39.570
at the University of Chicago--

00:28:39.570 --> 00:28:42.970
at the Stagg athletic field--

00:28:42.970 --> 00:28:47.640
that, indeed, you could
have a cascading system--

00:28:47.640 --> 00:28:55.380
a reproducing system--
that you can think of.

00:28:55.380 --> 00:29:00.690
The idea was to collect enough
uranium and enough graphite

00:29:00.690 --> 00:29:02.740
together.

00:29:02.740 --> 00:29:07.610
The graphite was to serve
as a moderating medium

00:29:07.610 --> 00:29:15.660
for the extra neutrons that came
from the uranium fissioning,

00:29:15.660 --> 00:29:19.530
and the graphite would
cascade the energy down

00:29:19.530 --> 00:29:23.310
until those neutrons would
then cause further fission

00:29:23.310 --> 00:29:26.260
events in other uranium.

00:29:26.260 --> 00:29:28.540
Giving, again, two
for every one--

00:29:28.540 --> 00:29:30.790
more than two for every one.

00:29:30.790 --> 00:29:34.690
And that process would
continue and build up.

00:29:34.690 --> 00:29:41.200
Well, the chance of success of
obtaining such a configuration,

00:29:41.200 --> 00:29:49.560
we recognize, would increase
as the physical size

00:29:49.560 --> 00:29:52.720
of this configuration
would get larger,

00:29:52.720 --> 00:29:57.130
because volume effects go
up as the cube of the size,

00:29:57.130 --> 00:30:00.070
whereas surface area--

00:30:00.070 --> 00:30:03.580
which determine
leakage from a system--

00:30:03.580 --> 00:30:06.940
go up only as a
square of the area.

00:30:06.940 --> 00:30:12.400
So there's an advantage
in increased size.

00:30:12.400 --> 00:30:15.700
So the test was
going to come when

00:30:15.700 --> 00:30:19.750
one could get a large enough
configuration of uranium

00:30:19.750 --> 00:30:24.200
and graphite collected
together into a system.

00:30:24.200 --> 00:30:28.400
This was obtained,
and it was detected.

00:30:28.400 --> 00:30:36.000
It was very carefully monitored
with sensitive equipment

00:30:36.000 --> 00:30:39.980
in this first experiment
at Stagg Field

00:30:39.980 --> 00:30:42.410
at the University of Chicago.

00:30:42.410 --> 00:30:47.120
That occurred in December 1942.

00:30:47.120 --> 00:30:51.750
December 1942.

00:30:51.750 --> 00:30:55.710
Once it had been demonstrated--
once Fermi had demonstrated

00:30:55.710 --> 00:31:01.830
that, indeed, you could have a
self-reacting system this way--

00:31:01.830 --> 00:31:04.380
the experiment was shut down.

00:31:04.380 --> 00:31:06.570
They didn't want it
to get out of control.

00:31:06.570 --> 00:31:10.020
Shut down, and
people immediately

00:31:10.020 --> 00:31:15.350
started to design
facilities for using that.

00:31:20.120 --> 00:31:25.650
The thinking at that time was to
construct a reacting system off

00:31:25.650 --> 00:31:32.220
somewhere else
that would produce

00:31:32.220 --> 00:31:35.940
some isotopes which were badly
needed during the war time

00:31:35.940 --> 00:31:36.960
years--

00:31:36.960 --> 00:31:41.340
most notably isotopes
of plutonium,

00:31:41.340 --> 00:31:45.570
which were considered to
be useful in atomic weapons

00:31:45.570 --> 00:31:47.470
production.

00:31:47.470 --> 00:31:53.040
So they wanted to build
this test reactor--

00:31:53.040 --> 00:31:56.940
which would operate at a
considerable power level now--

00:31:56.940 --> 00:32:04.230
as a production test facility
for even larger facilities

00:32:04.230 --> 00:32:07.170
which were to be
built elsewhere.

00:32:07.170 --> 00:32:11.610
It was decided to
position this test reactor

00:32:11.610 --> 00:32:14.090
at Oak Ridge, Tennessee--

00:32:14.090 --> 00:32:17.430
when the army had absorbed
a large area there

00:32:17.430 --> 00:32:20.590
for industrial use.

00:32:20.590 --> 00:32:25.290
And this reactor
was put on paper.

00:32:25.290 --> 00:32:27.870
Construction was
carried through,

00:32:27.870 --> 00:32:34.230
and was completed in an
amazing period of 11 months--

00:32:34.230 --> 00:32:40.230
December 1942-- until the
first use of the reactor

00:32:40.230 --> 00:32:43.570
in November 1943.

00:32:43.570 --> 00:32:46.770
11 months in total.

00:32:46.770 --> 00:32:50.620
And this was all 500
miles away from Chicago,

00:32:50.620 --> 00:32:54.100
where the first experiments
were carried out.

00:32:54.100 --> 00:32:57.510
So a whole flock
of physicists were

00:32:57.510 --> 00:33:01.910
conveyed and sent from Chicago
down to the Oak Ridge area--

00:33:01.910 --> 00:33:06.300
and engineers galore,
and construction people.

00:33:06.300 --> 00:33:11.790
And this amazing development
was pushed through.

00:33:11.790 --> 00:33:13.980
So this reactor
started to operate

00:33:13.980 --> 00:33:19.230
at Oak Ridge in November 1943.

00:33:19.230 --> 00:33:24.190
And I'll show, shortly,
a photograph of that.

00:33:24.190 --> 00:33:27.450
But let me first project this.

00:33:38.860 --> 00:33:43.630
This is a chronological
listing of the starting periods

00:33:43.630 --> 00:33:46.720
of the very early piles--

00:33:46.720 --> 00:33:51.780
they were called
piles back "Reactor"

00:33:51.780 --> 00:33:56.800
as a term came into
vogue later on.

00:33:56.800 --> 00:33:58.530
Piles.

00:33:58.530 --> 00:34:02.410
This Oak Ridge graphite pile--
which I was just describing its

00:34:02.410 --> 00:34:07.160
start-up date was in 1943--

00:34:07.160 --> 00:34:10.510
at the same time as the
construction of this reactor

00:34:10.510 --> 00:34:11.889
was going on--

00:34:11.889 --> 00:34:13.690
designing and construction.

00:34:13.690 --> 00:34:17.949
Another one was under
construction back in Chicago,

00:34:17.949 --> 00:34:22.090
in what was then called
the Argonne Laboratory.

00:34:22.090 --> 00:34:26.489
And it differed from the
Oak Ridge graphite pile.

00:34:26.489 --> 00:34:31.710
This should have actually been
written Clinton graphite pile.

00:34:31.710 --> 00:34:34.679
Clinton is a town in
Tennessee which is the nearest

00:34:34.679 --> 00:34:40.110
town to the Oak Ridge facility.

00:34:40.110 --> 00:34:42.219
So at that time,
this was all Clinton,

00:34:42.219 --> 00:34:46.159
and Oak Ridge came in later.

00:34:46.159 --> 00:34:49.824
The Argonne reactor was to
differ from the graphite

00:34:49.824 --> 00:34:55.290
pile in the Oak
Ridge area by having

00:34:55.290 --> 00:35:00.040
heavy water instead of graphite
as a moderating medium.

00:35:00.040 --> 00:35:03.690
The heavy water was to
slow down the neutrons

00:35:03.690 --> 00:35:07.440
and cause, again,
fissioning in uranium--

00:35:07.440 --> 00:35:12.390
the same as in this first
pile, which had graphite

00:35:12.390 --> 00:35:15.090
as the moderating medium.

00:35:15.090 --> 00:35:20.760
At later times, in '47,
the Chalk River reactor

00:35:20.760 --> 00:35:23.190
came into operation.

00:35:23.190 --> 00:35:26.160
And this is the one
where Dr. Brockhouse

00:35:26.160 --> 00:35:28.770
did all his research work--

00:35:28.770 --> 00:35:32.430
up at this Canadian
government facility.

00:35:32.430 --> 00:35:34.370
And then later ones in England.

00:35:34.370 --> 00:35:37.800
This is at England, and
another one at Oak Ridge,

00:35:37.800 --> 00:35:43.650
and at Brookhaven on Long
Island in this country.

00:35:43.650 --> 00:35:48.060
My next projection shows a
photograph of this graphite

00:35:48.060 --> 00:35:50.000
reactor at Oak Ridge.

00:35:56.180 --> 00:36:01.550
The reactor is a
very large assembly--

00:36:01.550 --> 00:36:07.430
a cubic assembly, roughly
20 feet on an edge--

00:36:07.430 --> 00:36:12.560
of stacked graphite-- pure
graphite-- components,

00:36:12.560 --> 00:36:15.890
what were called
graphite stringers.

00:36:15.890 --> 00:36:23.660
So these graphite stringers were
square, configurational chunks

00:36:23.660 --> 00:36:26.660
of pure graphite, and
that was a problem

00:36:26.660 --> 00:36:31.130
to prepare a graphite
of sufficient purity.

00:36:31.130 --> 00:36:37.970
About 4 or 5 inches on a side,
and 3 or 4 feet in length.

00:36:37.970 --> 00:36:42.320
Rectangular assemblies,
just stacked together.

00:36:42.320 --> 00:36:46.850
On the corners of these graphite
sections-- graphite pieces--

00:36:46.850 --> 00:36:51.470
there was a diagonal cut.

00:36:51.470 --> 00:36:55.280
And so when these elements
became stacked together,

00:36:55.280 --> 00:36:59.150
there would be channels
running down along the length.

00:36:59.150 --> 00:37:07.400
In these channels, one was to
place the uranium fissioning

00:37:07.400 --> 00:37:09.300
fuel.

00:37:09.300 --> 00:37:13.100
The uranium was
pure metal uranium.

00:37:13.100 --> 00:37:18.140
Many problems associated with
preparing a pure uranium.

00:37:18.140 --> 00:37:21.800
And the uranium
had to be encased,

00:37:21.800 --> 00:37:25.130
because uranium will oxidize.

00:37:25.130 --> 00:37:26.960
And if it gets hot--

00:37:26.960 --> 00:37:30.360
which it would in
this operation--

00:37:30.360 --> 00:37:32.600
then it would
oxidize very rapidly

00:37:32.600 --> 00:37:36.770
and become uranium oxide
and become a powder,

00:37:36.770 --> 00:37:39.560
and essentially collapse.

00:37:39.560 --> 00:37:44.390
So each of these
uranium pieces, which

00:37:44.390 --> 00:37:49.150
are roughly an inch in
diameter and 5 inches long--

00:37:49.150 --> 00:37:51.350
an inch in diameter,
5 inches long--

00:37:51.350 --> 00:37:56.450
had to be encased in
an aluminum shielding

00:37:56.450 --> 00:38:02.360
can, and sealed up to prevent
air from getting to the uranium

00:38:02.360 --> 00:38:04.950
and oxidizing the uranium.

00:38:04.950 --> 00:38:06.740
So we think of this
configuration--

00:38:06.740 --> 00:38:10.340
of all this graphite,
tons of it--

00:38:10.340 --> 00:38:12.330
stacked up.

00:38:12.330 --> 00:38:15.450
Not bolted together, or
not clamped together,

00:38:15.450 --> 00:38:21.420
because the graphite would
become hot and expand,

00:38:21.420 --> 00:38:28.320
and any banding or
clamping might be broken.

00:38:28.320 --> 00:38:32.310
And so all of this
graphite with these uranium

00:38:32.310 --> 00:38:36.340
rods running through
the assembly.

00:38:36.340 --> 00:38:37.240
Much uranium.

00:38:37.240 --> 00:38:40.540
I don't have a figure for
the mass of the uranium

00:38:40.540 --> 00:38:45.010
or the graphite involved,
but you see the scale of it.

00:38:45.010 --> 00:38:49.120
Now, all of that assembly had
to be surrounded by a shield

00:38:49.120 --> 00:38:52.450
to prevent radiation
from escaping out

00:38:52.450 --> 00:38:56.770
to where people might be
operating the assembly

00:38:56.770 --> 00:38:59.260
or doing something outside.

00:38:59.260 --> 00:39:02.020
So it had to be
encased in a concrete--

00:39:02.020 --> 00:39:06.850
essentially, a concrete
house the whole way around,

00:39:06.850 --> 00:39:10.660
the concrete being about
5 feet in thickness.

00:39:10.660 --> 00:39:13.390
5 feet in thickness.

00:39:13.390 --> 00:39:17.320
So we see sections
of that around here.

00:39:17.320 --> 00:39:20.050
We're looking at what's
called the loading

00:39:20.050 --> 00:39:22.630
face of this assembly.

00:39:22.630 --> 00:39:30.400
You can perhaps see vestiges of
the channels that are traveling

00:39:30.400 --> 00:39:33.790
back away from us in the photo.

00:39:33.790 --> 00:39:37.810
That would represent positions
where the uranium rods were

00:39:37.810 --> 00:39:45.070
placed, and this loading face
is where those radium rods would

00:39:45.070 --> 00:39:50.710
have been originally pushed
back into the graphite assembly.

00:39:50.710 --> 00:39:54.250
So there's a loading
platform here that's

00:39:54.250 --> 00:39:56.740
on a hydraulic crane assembly.

00:39:56.740 --> 00:39:58.170
Can go up and down.

00:39:58.170 --> 00:40:01.390
Here are a couple fellas doing
something at the front face,

00:40:01.390 --> 00:40:02.080
there.

00:40:02.080 --> 00:40:07.360
Perhaps pushing some uranium
on back through the assembly.

00:40:07.360 --> 00:40:10.210
And the control room
is over in here.

00:40:10.210 --> 00:40:14.320
And for no particular reason,
they can look at the front face

00:40:14.320 --> 00:40:15.320
here.

00:40:15.320 --> 00:40:18.380
But there had to be shielding
on this front face as well.

00:40:18.380 --> 00:40:21.310
Once you had completed
an operation in an area,

00:40:21.310 --> 00:40:23.480
you had to shield that off.

00:40:23.480 --> 00:40:25.870
The main shielding
was all around here.

00:40:25.870 --> 00:40:28.840
At the back side
of the reactor--

00:40:28.840 --> 00:40:32.590
the backside-- one
could push these rods--

00:40:32.590 --> 00:40:35.170
the uranium rods--
through, and they

00:40:35.170 --> 00:40:39.970
would fall down into
a plenum chamber

00:40:39.970 --> 00:40:44.580
and could be collected
together and transported

00:40:44.580 --> 00:40:48.880
with remote control facilities
over to a chemical processing

00:40:48.880 --> 00:40:51.970
plant which is next door.

00:40:51.970 --> 00:40:54.670
The purpose of this
reactor was to produce

00:40:54.670 --> 00:40:58.900
the first viable
quantities of plutonium.

00:40:58.900 --> 00:41:01.270
One of the isotopes
of uranium, when

00:41:01.270 --> 00:41:04.210
it's subjected to this
neutron radiation,

00:41:04.210 --> 00:41:07.660
transforms itself
into a plutonium--

00:41:07.660 --> 00:41:12.790
the next element up in the
periodic table from uranium--

00:41:12.790 --> 00:41:15.550
into a plutonium isotope.

00:41:15.550 --> 00:41:22.390
And from previous measurements
and studies of that isotope,

00:41:22.390 --> 00:41:27.970
that was potentially a very
good atomic weapon component.

00:41:27.970 --> 00:41:29.650
Recall we're in the middle--

00:41:29.650 --> 00:41:34.300
in the starting period of
the war-- and everybody

00:41:34.300 --> 00:41:38.080
was desperate for new
developments, here.

00:41:38.080 --> 00:41:41.440
So the primary
purpose of the reactor

00:41:41.440 --> 00:41:44.560
was to produce
sizable quantities

00:41:44.560 --> 00:41:50.560
that the chemists could get at
and purify and study processes,

00:41:50.560 --> 00:41:53.630
and the physicists could
get out and study processes

00:41:53.630 --> 00:42:03.120
with the plutonium for
weapons potential use.

00:42:03.120 --> 00:42:07.740
So there was no experimentation
as such with all this neutron

00:42:07.740 --> 00:42:12.210
radiation which was bouncing
around inside this facility.

00:42:12.210 --> 00:42:16.990
There was one experiment that
I know of that was performed,

00:42:16.990 --> 00:42:21.740
and that dealt with, again,
producing information

00:42:21.740 --> 00:42:24.970
for further use on the project--

00:42:24.970 --> 00:42:28.700
what was called a
pile oscillator.

00:42:28.700 --> 00:42:31.140
A pile oscillator.

00:42:31.140 --> 00:42:34.080
Something moves back and forth.

00:42:34.080 --> 00:42:38.160
The idea is to have a
detector back inside a section

00:42:38.160 --> 00:42:40.450
of this configuration--

00:42:40.450 --> 00:42:43.170
a neutron detector.

00:42:43.170 --> 00:42:47.040
And you move a sample
that you want to study

00:42:47.040 --> 00:42:50.610
back and forth in its vicinity.

00:42:50.610 --> 00:42:56.250
You can determine, with
this device, the capture

00:42:56.250 --> 00:42:59.760
or absorption cross-section
of this medium

00:42:59.760 --> 00:43:01.900
for neutron radiation.

00:43:01.900 --> 00:43:07.200
And that was of great
interest in the technology

00:43:07.200 --> 00:43:09.030
at that time--

00:43:09.030 --> 00:43:17.490
again, for the practical uses to
which the project was devoted.

00:43:17.490 --> 00:43:20.970
So there was that one
experimental facility,

00:43:20.970 --> 00:43:24.570
and that's the only use of
the neutrons that I know of,

00:43:24.570 --> 00:43:27.330
other than to continue
the fissioning

00:43:27.330 --> 00:43:30.050
process in the uranium
inside the assembly.

00:43:32.580 --> 00:43:37.830
Well, this was used throughout
the war for producing--

00:43:37.830 --> 00:43:42.610
war years, until the war
ended in the summer of 1945--

00:43:42.610 --> 00:43:46.080
for producing
quantities of plutonium.

00:43:46.080 --> 00:43:51.450
As soon as this
reactor was operating,

00:43:51.450 --> 00:43:55.830
new reactors were under
consideration for construction

00:43:55.830 --> 00:43:58.040
out in the state of Washington--

00:43:58.040 --> 00:43:58.750
at Hanford.

00:44:01.620 --> 00:44:04.160
Much larger reactors.

00:44:04.160 --> 00:44:07.920
Much larger piles,
I should say--

00:44:07.920 --> 00:44:10.680
which worked to produce
more abundant, more

00:44:10.680 --> 00:44:14.520
copious quantities of plutonium.

00:44:14.520 --> 00:44:17.630
So those were under
construction out there

00:44:17.630 --> 00:44:23.790
when this was operating
for in the first year.

00:44:23.790 --> 00:44:29.340
But this reactor continued
to operate in that way.

00:44:29.340 --> 00:44:32.700
The total power
production in this reactor

00:44:32.700 --> 00:44:36.060
was about 3,500 watts--

00:44:36.060 --> 00:44:39.090
3,500 kilowatts, excuse me.

00:44:39.090 --> 00:44:41.430
3,500 kilowatts.

00:44:41.430 --> 00:44:46.410
3 or 4 megawatts of
power production.

00:44:46.410 --> 00:44:50.760
Present day power reactors
operate roughly at 100

00:44:50.760 --> 00:44:53.010
megawatts--

00:44:53.010 --> 00:44:58.830
25 or 30 times the operating
power level of this assembly.

00:44:58.830 --> 00:45:03.580
I didn't mention-- how do you
get this power out of here?

00:45:03.580 --> 00:45:07.440
Well, the power release
was to be obtained

00:45:07.440 --> 00:45:12.600
by having air stream
along these channels that

00:45:12.600 --> 00:45:19.260
surround the uranium
plugs that were permeating

00:45:19.260 --> 00:45:20.880
the whole assembly--

00:45:20.880 --> 00:45:24.310
having airstream along there.

00:45:24.310 --> 00:45:30.280
So you needed big fans in
the exit side to draw air in.

00:45:30.280 --> 00:45:32.460
So all through this
building, there

00:45:32.460 --> 00:45:36.480
was a sucking sound of
air being pulled in here

00:45:36.480 --> 00:45:38.220
and cooling the reactor.

00:45:38.220 --> 00:45:42.720
Taking this 3
megawatts of power--

00:45:42.720 --> 00:45:47.070
thermal power-- out
and warming up the air,

00:45:47.070 --> 00:45:54.030
and then the air was
filtered and sent up stacks.

00:45:54.030 --> 00:45:59.700
Well, at the end
of the war years--

00:45:59.700 --> 00:46:07.925
at the end of the war,
in the summer of 1945--

00:46:07.925 --> 00:46:14.520
one of the research scientists
at the laboratory here,

00:46:14.520 --> 00:46:18.780
Ernest Wollan, who had come
down with the original group

00:46:18.780 --> 00:46:20.550
from Chicago--

00:46:20.550 --> 00:46:24.390
Ernest Wollan-- got to
thinking seriously--

00:46:24.390 --> 00:46:25.950
as other people did--

00:46:25.950 --> 00:46:33.810
how can you use these
facilities as research tools?

00:46:33.810 --> 00:46:38.910
And Ernie Wollan decided
to see if he couldn't

00:46:38.910 --> 00:46:43.140
make use of some of these
copious neutrons that

00:46:43.140 --> 00:46:47.220
were rattling around in here,
trying to bring some out

00:46:47.220 --> 00:46:50.460
through a collimated
tube, and trying

00:46:50.460 --> 00:46:56.370
to exploit this diffractive
property of the neutron

00:46:56.370 --> 00:46:57.900
radiation.

00:46:57.900 --> 00:47:02.610
So he decided he wanted to
try to set up a diffraction

00:47:02.610 --> 00:47:07.380
spectrometer in the way that
X-ray people had been doing

00:47:07.380 --> 00:47:11.160
for 15 or 20 years earlier--

00:47:11.160 --> 00:47:15.570
bringing a beam out and
defracting it from samples,

00:47:15.570 --> 00:47:22.410
seeing what novel things might
be available from such studies.

00:47:22.410 --> 00:47:26.400
So Ernie arranged
to have sent down

00:47:26.400 --> 00:47:32.100
from the University of Chicago
an old X-ray spectrometer unit

00:47:32.100 --> 00:47:34.650
that he had used back up there.

00:47:34.650 --> 00:47:40.440
He did his thesis work in an
earlier time with Compton,

00:47:40.440 --> 00:47:45.760
studying X-ray scattering
by gas samples.

00:47:45.760 --> 00:47:49.260
So in that, he had built
an X-ray spectrometer,

00:47:49.260 --> 00:47:52.080
and he knew that
was still up there,

00:47:52.080 --> 00:47:54.050
and he arranged to
have that sent down

00:47:54.050 --> 00:48:01.470
to Oak Ridge in
the fall of 1945.

00:48:01.470 --> 00:48:06.550
So he and a colleague
of his, Robert Sawyer--

00:48:06.550 --> 00:48:09.890
who was there during
parts of the war years,

00:48:09.890 --> 00:48:11.755
from University Lehigh--

00:48:15.390 --> 00:48:18.420
configured this
spectrometer so that they

00:48:18.420 --> 00:48:22.600
could try to bring a neutron
beam out from the reactor,

00:48:22.600 --> 00:48:29.070
now, off to a side, and have
it fall upon this spectrometer.

00:48:29.070 --> 00:48:31.030
Which, a spectrometer,
in general,

00:48:31.030 --> 00:48:34.980
is something that you can
control angular positions.

00:48:34.980 --> 00:48:38.970
Have a detector on it, and you
can move the detector around

00:48:38.970 --> 00:48:41.760
and determine a
pattern of scattering,

00:48:41.760 --> 00:48:44.800
or a pattern of diffraction.

00:48:44.800 --> 00:48:52.300
So that was the idea
in this first effort.

00:48:52.300 --> 00:48:57.780
And his spectrometer, I show
in my next projection, here.

00:49:06.100 --> 00:49:09.490
This was the original
spectrometer.

00:49:09.490 --> 00:49:13.960
The reactor is behind
here, off to the back side.

00:49:13.960 --> 00:49:17.290
And there has not
been a channel--

00:49:17.290 --> 00:49:21.820
an open channel-- through this
graphite assembly that lets

00:49:21.820 --> 00:49:24.280
neutrons stream out--

00:49:24.280 --> 00:49:27.250
those that happen to be coming
in the right direction--

00:49:27.250 --> 00:49:32.290
and stream out
through the shield

00:49:32.290 --> 00:49:35.500
and the face of the reactor.

00:49:35.500 --> 00:49:38.500
Well, they have to be
contained, so there's

00:49:38.500 --> 00:49:42.620
another outside shield
built up around here.

00:49:42.620 --> 00:49:45.640
So the idea was to
place a monochromating

00:49:45.640 --> 00:49:48.250
crystal in that beam.

00:49:48.250 --> 00:49:51.490
Neutrons were coming
out and falling

00:49:51.490 --> 00:49:56.090
on a large, single
crystal of some sort,

00:49:56.090 --> 00:50:01.370
and being diffracted
from that single crystal.

00:50:01.370 --> 00:50:05.840
Even if these were different
energy neutrons, as they are--

00:50:05.840 --> 00:50:09.680
thermal neutrons
with a thermal--

00:50:09.680 --> 00:50:14.540
one wants to select out
a monochromatic section

00:50:14.540 --> 00:50:16.730
of that spectrum--

00:50:16.730 --> 00:50:20.330
a monoenergetic section.

00:50:20.330 --> 00:50:23.960
And that can be done by
diffraction from a crystal.

00:50:23.960 --> 00:50:25.520
One wavelength.

00:50:25.520 --> 00:50:27.620
One energy.

00:50:27.620 --> 00:50:31.280
So back inside this
secondary shield

00:50:31.280 --> 00:50:34.110
was this monochromating crystal.

00:50:34.110 --> 00:50:37.430
Now, about the only
large, sizable crystal

00:50:37.430 --> 00:50:42.740
that was available to Ernie
and Sawyer at the time

00:50:42.740 --> 00:50:44.810
was sodium chloride.

00:50:44.810 --> 00:50:48.530
Sodium chloride
seems everywhere.

00:50:48.530 --> 00:50:50.750
And people had to
learn the technique

00:50:50.750 --> 00:50:55.890
of preparing the large
crystals of sodium chloride.

00:50:55.890 --> 00:51:01.480
The infrared people were
hot on alkali crystals--

00:51:04.100 --> 00:51:10.000
very fine, infrared
window material.

00:51:10.000 --> 00:51:14.300
And so the development people
had developed the technique

00:51:14.300 --> 00:51:19.610
for preparing large
crystals of alkali halides--

00:51:19.610 --> 00:51:22.400
most notably, sodium chloride.

00:51:22.400 --> 00:51:26.830
So Ernie obtained a
sizable single crystal

00:51:26.830 --> 00:51:31.250
of sodium chloride,
roughly an inch and a half

00:51:31.250 --> 00:51:37.350
high and 2 inches long,
and a 1/2 inch thick--

00:51:37.350 --> 00:51:43.580
which is an ungodly size for
a single crystal of substance

00:51:43.580 --> 00:51:45.840
back in those days--

00:51:45.840 --> 00:51:49.790
and used that as a
monochromating crystal in here.

00:51:49.790 --> 00:51:52.670
From that monochromating
crystal, one would have,

00:51:52.670 --> 00:51:57.170
then, a pure diffractive
beam from it coming out,

00:51:57.170 --> 00:52:01.850
and that came out through
this secondary shield, crossed

00:52:01.850 --> 00:52:04.760
over the axis of
this spectrometer,

00:52:04.760 --> 00:52:06.940
about which I've spoken.

00:52:06.940 --> 00:52:11.130
Here is this old, original,
University of Chicago

00:52:11.130 --> 00:52:16.611
X-ray spectrometer bolted down
to the platform on the floor,

00:52:16.611 --> 00:52:17.110
here.

00:52:19.770 --> 00:52:21.660
And this neutron beam--

00:52:21.660 --> 00:52:23.880
purified neutron beam, now--

00:52:23.880 --> 00:52:30.130
coming out and crossing the
axis of this spectrometer.

00:52:30.130 --> 00:52:34.570
One would place, then, a
further scattering sample

00:52:34.570 --> 00:52:38.870
or diffracting
sample on this axis

00:52:38.870 --> 00:52:42.880
so that the beam would come
in and strike the sample,

00:52:42.880 --> 00:52:48.760
and now be diffracted
around from that sample.

00:52:48.760 --> 00:52:51.870
So here is a detector.

00:52:51.870 --> 00:52:55.720
This is the most massive
thing on the assembly.

00:52:55.720 --> 00:52:59.130
The detector itself
is not large.

00:52:59.130 --> 00:53:06.450
It's about 2 inches in diameter
and about 15 inches long,

00:53:06.450 --> 00:53:13.800
and it's a sealed chamber
sealed with an insulated wire

00:53:13.800 --> 00:53:17.610
along the center, and the
two electrodes, the wire,

00:53:17.610 --> 00:53:21.790
and the case, and filled
with a special gas--

00:53:21.790 --> 00:53:24.640
boron trifluoride.

00:53:24.640 --> 00:53:27.280
Boron is the important thing.

00:53:27.280 --> 00:53:32.150
Neutrons are absorbed
copiously in boron,

00:53:32.150 --> 00:53:34.870
and so the idea was
that a neutron coming

00:53:34.870 --> 00:53:39.730
in along the axis
of this chamber

00:53:39.730 --> 00:53:42.880
would be at least
partially absorbed

00:53:42.880 --> 00:53:46.870
by boron nuclei in
this gas, and that

00:53:46.870 --> 00:53:51.130
would trigger an electrical
response between the wire

00:53:51.130 --> 00:53:54.220
and the piece,
which were charged

00:53:54.220 --> 00:53:58.970
to a high potential
difference between the two.

00:53:58.970 --> 00:54:02.620
So the chamber is inside
here, and the surrounding here

00:54:02.620 --> 00:54:05.180
is secondary shielding.

00:54:05.180 --> 00:54:09.860
The shielding is the massive
part of the assembly, here.

00:54:09.860 --> 00:54:14.150
All of this shielding to
keep stray radiation away

00:54:14.150 --> 00:54:15.680
from that detector.

00:54:15.680 --> 00:54:20.000
But this whole assembly can
be moved around the axis

00:54:20.000 --> 00:54:21.950
of the spectrometer.

00:54:21.950 --> 00:54:24.620
Now, this weighs--
this has a mass--

00:54:24.620 --> 00:54:28.640
much larger than this
spectrometer shafting

00:54:28.640 --> 00:54:30.200
can support.

00:54:30.200 --> 00:54:33.700
So to take off some
of this loading,

00:54:33.700 --> 00:54:36.530
there were cables running
up to a gimbal joint up

00:54:36.530 --> 00:54:42.830
on the ceiling, here, so
that this off-axis loading--

00:54:42.830 --> 00:54:46.830
mass loading-- was supported
from this universal gimbal

00:54:46.830 --> 00:54:49.190
joint up on top, here.

00:54:49.190 --> 00:54:51.940
Well, this had to be
aligned pretty well.

00:54:51.940 --> 00:54:56.150
And I remember, after I
came into the picture, all

00:54:56.150 --> 00:54:58.940
the troubles that we had
in keeping it aligned--

00:54:58.940 --> 00:55:02.690
making sure that this
operation of angular position

00:55:02.690 --> 00:55:05.990
here was a smooth operation.

00:55:05.990 --> 00:55:09.440
But here is shown on here,
now, a typical sample--

00:55:09.440 --> 00:55:15.300
a sample in this
circular sample.

00:55:15.300 --> 00:55:17.120
And this is a powder sample--

00:55:17.120 --> 00:55:21.760
random crystallite
direction within the powder.

00:55:21.760 --> 00:55:26.710
Sealed up between aluminum
windows in a plate.

00:55:26.710 --> 00:55:30.570
The sample thickness-- you
don't get the impression here--

00:55:30.570 --> 00:55:34.050
is perhaps 1/2 inch thick.

00:55:34.050 --> 00:55:36.620
The neutrons had to go
through perhaps 1/2 inch

00:55:36.620 --> 00:55:42.570
thickness of the powder sample
in the diffracting process.

00:55:42.570 --> 00:55:45.685
And the ceiling is just to
keep the powder in there.

00:55:45.685 --> 00:55:47.550
They're aluminum windows, there.

00:55:47.550 --> 00:55:52.500
Aluminum is very transparent
to neutron radiation.

00:55:52.500 --> 00:55:55.820
In operation, here, one
had to turn handles.

00:55:55.820 --> 00:55:59.850
There are some handles, here,
turning the detector around.

00:55:59.850 --> 00:56:05.340
Also, separately, controlling
the angular position

00:56:05.340 --> 00:56:07.350
of the sample.

00:56:07.350 --> 00:56:10.920
It was all completely
hand-operated.

00:56:10.920 --> 00:56:13.380
If you wanted to study the
scattering around here,

00:56:13.380 --> 00:56:16.650
you positioned
this to some point

00:56:16.650 --> 00:56:20.340
and counted neutrons
that were being

00:56:20.340 --> 00:56:23.730
detected in this detector
over some period.

00:56:23.730 --> 00:56:26.550
That period might be
as short as a minute.

00:56:26.550 --> 00:56:30.520
It might be as long as
10 minutes or 15 minutes.

00:56:30.520 --> 00:56:35.010
And then after a conclusion
of obtaining that information,

00:56:35.010 --> 00:56:40.430
go and turn to a new position
and continue the process.

00:56:40.430 --> 00:56:47.480
Powder diffracting patterns
were very time-consuming

00:56:47.480 --> 00:56:51.750
in their obtainment.

00:56:55.170 --> 00:56:58.870
Well, with this
sort of assembly,

00:56:58.870 --> 00:57:03.690
Ernie and Sawyer
indeed found that they

00:57:03.690 --> 00:57:10.200
could take powdered
diffractions from specimens.

00:57:10.200 --> 00:57:18.420
And I show here a powder
pattern obtained with a sample

00:57:18.420 --> 00:57:21.450
of, again, sodium chloride now.

00:57:21.450 --> 00:57:24.210
Sodium chloride powder.

00:57:24.210 --> 00:57:27.420
There's a sodium chloride
monochromator way up in there.

00:57:27.420 --> 00:57:28.750
That's a single crystal.

00:57:28.750 --> 00:57:30.990
This is now the powder sample.

00:57:30.990 --> 00:57:36.140
This is intensity versus
scattering angle position.

00:57:36.140 --> 00:57:41.940
This was presented in a monthly
project report at the Clinton

00:57:41.940 --> 00:57:45.930
Laboratory in April 1946.

00:57:45.930 --> 00:57:48.900
The assembly was
collected together

00:57:48.900 --> 00:57:56.730
in late 1945 and early
1946 by Wollan and Sawyer.

00:57:56.730 --> 00:58:00.000
And here are a couple
of diffraction peaks.

00:58:00.000 --> 00:58:04.110
So these are nice,
well-behaved looking peaks

00:58:04.110 --> 00:58:05.750
with measurable intensity.

00:58:05.750 --> 00:58:12.630
They take time, and it was a
chore to obtain such things,

00:58:12.630 --> 00:58:15.270
but it was available.

00:58:15.270 --> 00:58:17.090
Notice the background
intensity--

00:58:17.090 --> 00:58:21.610
the baseline intensity-- that's
underneath all this structure.

00:58:21.610 --> 00:58:29.206
There's more of it out here, but
patience ran out at this time.

00:58:29.206 --> 00:58:31.440
But notice the
level of background

00:58:31.440 --> 00:58:34.710
versus signal intensity, here.

00:58:34.710 --> 00:58:39.960
The background is as much
or larger than the signal,

00:58:39.960 --> 00:58:44.800
and that's a
troublesome feature,

00:58:44.800 --> 00:58:48.910
and you'd like to do
something about it.

00:58:48.910 --> 00:58:52.850
Here's another of the
very earliest patterns.

00:58:52.850 --> 00:58:57.951
Ernie decided to look at water--

00:58:57.951 --> 00:58:58.450
H2O.

00:59:01.210 --> 00:59:03.640
And not only water,
but heavy water--

00:59:03.640 --> 00:59:09.300
D2O, and here are patterns
he and Sawyer had obtained

00:59:09.300 --> 00:59:11.350
in those very early days.

00:59:11.350 --> 00:59:14.300
Now, the distinction between
deuterium-- heavy water

00:59:14.300 --> 00:59:16.510
and light, normal water.

00:59:16.510 --> 00:59:21.530
The interest here was to see
if one could possibly determine

00:59:21.530 --> 00:59:24.490
steady, hydrogenous materials--

00:59:24.490 --> 00:59:29.410
hydrogen-containing
materials-- because--

00:59:29.410 --> 00:59:34.340
and this is very important--
such information--

00:59:34.340 --> 00:59:37.190
establishing the location
of hydrogen atoms

00:59:37.190 --> 00:59:40.870
in materials and
material structures--

00:59:40.870 --> 00:59:45.200
was completely not
available with X-radiation.

00:59:45.200 --> 00:59:50.720
Nobody had determined what the
real structure of normal water

00:59:50.720 --> 00:59:55.340
was, or frozen water or
any other hydrogenous

00:59:55.340 --> 01:00:00.650
material, because X-rays
were insensitive to hydrogen

01:00:00.650 --> 01:00:03.110
in hydrogen scattering.

01:00:03.110 --> 01:00:07.190
That was not
expected, but it was

01:00:07.190 --> 01:00:10.140
hoped that maybe with
neutron radiation,

01:00:10.140 --> 01:00:13.250
there would be a difference
enough between deuterium

01:00:13.250 --> 01:00:16.170
and hydrogen to
show a distinction.

01:00:16.170 --> 01:00:19.220
Well, these first
patterns-- which, there was

01:00:19.220 --> 01:00:21.680
no attempt at analyzing these.

01:00:21.680 --> 01:00:26.210
But they did show differences,
here, of this type

01:00:26.210 --> 01:00:28.010
that you see.

01:00:28.010 --> 01:00:29.670
This peak-- you're peaking.

01:00:29.670 --> 01:00:32.390
And these are liquid
patterns, so you don't

01:00:32.390 --> 01:00:35.210
expect sharp diffraction lines.

01:00:35.210 --> 01:00:40.220
But this peak is considerably
more prominent than

01:00:40.220 --> 01:00:43.310
for deuterium--
the heavy water--

01:00:43.310 --> 01:00:45.530
than for the
normal, light water.

01:00:45.530 --> 01:00:47.390
So this suggests that, again--

01:00:47.390 --> 01:00:50.120
that, indeed,
hydrogen and deuterium

01:00:50.120 --> 01:00:52.970
are scattering
differently, and that one

01:00:52.970 --> 01:00:57.470
is sensing that in these
diffraction patterns.

01:00:57.470 --> 01:01:00.740
Well, there was no analysis
of this at the time,

01:01:00.740 --> 01:01:07.030
but that was the purpose
of the experiments.

01:01:07.030 --> 01:01:12.350
Again, this was reported in this
same project progress report--

01:01:12.350 --> 01:01:15.080
that I had shown earlier--

01:01:15.080 --> 01:01:19.750
in April 1946.

01:01:19.750 --> 01:01:25.940
I came to the laboratory
in June of 1946,

01:01:25.940 --> 01:01:30.900
a month or two after this
period, joined the group.

01:01:30.900 --> 01:01:34.310
And Wollan and Sawyer
decided he was going

01:01:34.310 --> 01:01:36.300
to return to the university.

01:01:36.300 --> 01:01:39.530
So he came and went
back to Lehigh.

01:01:39.530 --> 01:01:43.330
And from then on, I
worked very closely

01:01:43.330 --> 01:01:49.960
with Ernie Wollan in
all of the developments.

01:01:49.960 --> 01:01:55.190
Well, knowing that
you could successfully

01:01:55.190 --> 01:01:59.330
take powder
diffraction patterns,

01:01:59.330 --> 01:02:04.130
then we just decided to
look at many materials,

01:02:04.130 --> 01:02:07.640
trying to make some
sense out of what was

01:02:07.640 --> 01:02:09.700
controlling these intensities.

01:02:09.700 --> 01:02:12.770
What were the
scattering properties

01:02:12.770 --> 01:02:17.300
of the different atoms for
this neutron radiation?

01:02:17.300 --> 01:02:19.640
There was something
known about that.

01:02:19.640 --> 01:02:25.440
There had been a compilation of
what is called total scattering

01:02:25.440 --> 01:02:31.700
cross-sections for a series
of different atomic nuclear

01:02:31.700 --> 01:02:33.740
species.

01:02:33.740 --> 01:02:37.550
But that's not the whole story.

01:02:37.550 --> 01:02:39.290
That's not the
whole thing that's

01:02:39.290 --> 01:02:44.970
going to determine these
powder peak intensities.

01:02:44.970 --> 01:02:52.170
It was recognized that
there are different isotopes

01:02:52.170 --> 01:02:53.910
for any element--

01:02:53.910 --> 01:02:55.830
for an element.

01:02:55.830 --> 01:02:59.610
Different isotopes
might very well

01:02:59.610 --> 01:03:04.760
display different
scattering properties.

01:03:04.760 --> 01:03:10.520
And eventually, one would
have to unravel all of that.

01:03:10.520 --> 01:03:17.640
Another thing-- many
nuclei have nuclear spin,

01:03:17.640 --> 01:03:19.410
and nuclear spin--

01:03:19.410 --> 01:03:26.110
if you have a nuclear spin, then
you have two nuclear spin state

01:03:26.110 --> 01:03:30.060
scattering amplitudes--

01:03:30.060 --> 01:03:33.280
one where the neutrons
come in this way,

01:03:33.280 --> 01:03:35.640
and one where the
neutrons come in this way

01:03:35.640 --> 01:03:36.740
to that nuclear spin.

01:03:39.670 --> 01:03:41.200
So what that implied--

01:03:41.200 --> 01:03:45.880
and in a general
sample, one would

01:03:45.880 --> 01:03:48.670
have these nuclei arrayed--
and random, of course.

01:03:48.670 --> 01:03:55.240
There was nothing to say the
spin should be up or down

01:03:55.240 --> 01:03:58.090
or whatever.

01:03:58.090 --> 01:04:01.650
Under those conditions,
what that implied--

01:04:01.650 --> 01:04:09.060
and the theory had predicted
this from the very earliest--

01:04:09.060 --> 01:04:14.340
that this, what we now call
the coherent scattering

01:04:14.340 --> 01:04:17.050
cross-section, should, indeed--

01:04:17.050 --> 01:04:21.550
or may, indeed-- differ from the
total scattering cross-section.

01:04:21.550 --> 01:04:24.400
We had information
on total scattering,

01:04:24.400 --> 01:04:29.450
but nothing at all on
coherent scattering.

01:04:29.450 --> 01:04:35.170
And the coherent scattering
arises and this difference

01:04:35.170 --> 01:04:39.240
comes in because of
this spin state--

01:04:39.240 --> 01:04:42.180
the possible difference
in these two spin

01:04:42.180 --> 01:04:48.130
state scattering cross-sections,
or scattering amplitudes.

01:04:48.130 --> 01:04:53.110
So in the long run, all of
that had to be unraveled,

01:04:53.110 --> 01:04:57.640
and nothing was known about
this distinction between what

01:04:57.640 --> 01:05:01.100
you were going to see
in a diffraction pattern

01:05:01.100 --> 01:05:04.500
and what would be predicted
from these total scattering

01:05:04.500 --> 01:05:06.010
cross-sections.

01:05:06.010 --> 01:05:08.720
You had to look specifically
at this quantity called

01:05:08.720 --> 01:05:12.580
the coherent scattering
cross-section,

01:05:12.580 --> 01:05:17.170
and that might be anything
from a very low value,

01:05:17.170 --> 01:05:21.460
up to the total
scattering cross-section.

01:05:21.460 --> 01:05:26.170
So that was the purpose
of looking at many

01:05:26.170 --> 01:05:28.420
of these early samples--

01:05:28.420 --> 01:05:33.550
to see if we could unravel and
understand these intensities

01:05:33.550 --> 01:05:37.120
and how they were affected
by cross-sections;

01:05:37.120 --> 01:05:42.670
and, indeed, what the
basic cross-sections were.

01:05:42.670 --> 01:05:46.540
Well, this was all very
choresome with this hand

01:05:46.540 --> 01:05:50.380
operation, and we
were all very anxious

01:05:50.380 --> 01:05:53.230
to try to improve
things so that we

01:05:53.230 --> 01:05:56.200
didn't have to sit
there through the night

01:05:56.200 --> 01:06:02.060
and collect the data by
hand and write things down.

01:06:02.060 --> 01:06:10.570
And we were very fortunate, in
1947, when a couple chaps came

01:06:10.570 --> 01:06:15.220
down to Oak Ridge as part of
what was called the Oak Ridge

01:06:15.220 --> 01:06:17.470
Training School.

01:06:17.470 --> 01:06:21.490
After the war, the
administration management

01:06:21.490 --> 01:06:24.820
decided to set up a
training school where

01:06:24.820 --> 01:06:28.570
they would encourage people to
come in there from universities

01:06:28.570 --> 01:06:31.720
or industrial
laboratories and learn,

01:06:31.720 --> 01:06:33.660
become familiar with
all the techniques

01:06:33.660 --> 01:06:37.510
and the new techniques which had
been developed during the war

01:06:37.510 --> 01:06:40.630
years, and which
were very secret.

01:06:40.630 --> 01:06:46.160
So these people were to come
in and talk with people, work

01:06:46.160 --> 01:06:49.030
in the laboratories
in many cases,

01:06:49.030 --> 01:06:51.430
but mostly to
attend lectures that

01:06:51.430 --> 01:06:54.070
were to be given by
experts who were brought in

01:06:54.070 --> 01:06:56.060
for that purpose.

01:06:56.060 --> 01:06:58.540
So this Oak Ridge Training
School-- which had, perhaps,

01:06:58.540 --> 01:07:03.650
150 students at some times--

01:07:03.650 --> 01:07:08.260
these were senior
people from universities

01:07:08.260 --> 01:07:12.310
or from industrial
laboratories that were sent

01:07:12.310 --> 01:07:14.440
to Oak Ridge for this purpose.

01:07:14.440 --> 01:07:18.610
And the name Frederick Seitz
may be familiar with you.

01:07:18.610 --> 01:07:25.060
Seitz, who was a very respected
solid state physicist,

01:07:25.060 --> 01:07:30.250
was brought there as Director of
this Oak Ridge Training School.

01:07:30.250 --> 01:07:33.310
Well, two of the
students that came in--

01:07:33.310 --> 01:07:37.600
one from the RCA
Laboratories in New Jersey,

01:07:37.600 --> 01:07:43.990
and the other from Goodrich Tire
Rubber Company over in Akron--

01:07:43.990 --> 01:07:50.010
George Morton from RCA and
Bill Davidson from Goodrich

01:07:50.010 --> 01:07:52.000
were two of the
students, and they

01:07:52.000 --> 01:07:57.080
became interested in these
neutron diffraction patterns.

01:07:57.080 --> 01:08:01.260
And in particular, Morton was
a very skilled instrumentation

01:08:01.260 --> 01:08:02.340
man.

01:08:02.340 --> 01:08:03.504
He had worked with--

01:08:06.780 --> 01:08:14.550
name's Zworykin, who was a
prime developer of electron

01:08:14.550 --> 01:08:20.680
microscopes in
former years, and RCA

01:08:20.680 --> 01:08:24.760
was in the business of
selling electron microscopes--

01:08:24.760 --> 01:08:28.050
first of the type.

01:08:28.050 --> 01:08:31.490
So Morton was a very
skilled experimentalist

01:08:31.490 --> 01:08:37.109
and instrumentation man.

01:08:37.109 --> 01:08:38.903
And he took on the task--

01:08:38.903 --> 01:08:40.319
between the two
of them, they took

01:08:40.319 --> 01:08:47.204
on the task of automatizing this
slow-operating spectrometer.

01:08:49.930 --> 01:08:52.420
And they made many improvements.

01:08:52.420 --> 01:08:55.160
We made many improvements,
there, this first year--

01:08:55.160 --> 01:08:58.359
in 1947.

01:08:58.359 --> 01:09:01.550
This is the same spectrometer.

01:09:01.550 --> 01:09:06.130
And now, about a year later
than in the earlier photo,

01:09:06.130 --> 01:09:07.740
here's the basic spectrometer.

01:09:07.740 --> 01:09:09.149
Nothing changed there.

01:09:09.149 --> 01:09:14.290
But now here's a nice,
automatic, control,

01:09:14.290 --> 01:09:16.950
automatic operating
system, here,

01:09:16.950 --> 01:09:21.600
that drives a worm,
here, with a clock motor

01:09:21.600 --> 01:09:27.540
and some cam wheels
with some microswitches.

01:09:27.540 --> 01:09:30.090
So that on demand,
this would now

01:09:30.090 --> 01:09:32.910
change its angular position.

01:09:32.910 --> 01:09:39.240
And at the same time, there
was a cadmium shutter assembly

01:09:39.240 --> 01:09:43.920
that could flop in or
out of the beam that

01:09:43.920 --> 01:09:46.529
was falling on the sample.

01:09:46.529 --> 01:09:49.470
And the electronics system
was improved over here

01:09:49.470 --> 01:09:50.955
on the control cabinet.

01:09:53.760 --> 01:10:01.340
And the detector-- there was
just general improvement things

01:10:01.340 --> 01:10:04.540
done to all of the components.

01:10:04.540 --> 01:10:10.080
Now we had a recorder
to print out data.

01:10:10.080 --> 01:10:14.490
We didn't have to be there
to see meters print out

01:10:14.490 --> 01:10:16.050
data recorder.

01:10:16.050 --> 01:10:19.560
This was what was called
a traffic counter.

01:10:19.560 --> 01:10:21.240
You've perhaps
seen these gimmicks

01:10:21.240 --> 01:10:24.180
along the highway, where they
have a tube running across,

01:10:24.180 --> 01:10:28.040
and gives a signal,
and this thing

01:10:28.040 --> 01:10:31.930
prints a new number over
here on the side of the road.

01:10:31.930 --> 01:10:35.910
That was the only
type of recorder--

01:10:35.910 --> 01:10:39.840
digital recorder-- that
we could find at the time.

01:10:39.840 --> 01:10:43.920
All the electronics
in the system--

01:10:43.920 --> 01:10:47.670
amplifiers, high-voltage
power supplies-- those

01:10:47.670 --> 01:10:49.810
all had to be made homemade.

01:10:49.810 --> 01:10:52.260
There was no commercial
instrumentation

01:10:52.260 --> 01:10:55.680
of that type available
in this period.

01:10:55.680 --> 01:10:58.320
So there were improvements
in these amplifiers.

01:10:58.320 --> 01:11:00.580
Vacuum tubes had gotten better.

01:11:00.580 --> 01:11:06.600
This was long before the day
of solid state electronics.

01:11:06.600 --> 01:11:10.800
Tubes had become
better and less noisy.

01:11:10.800 --> 01:11:13.280
But still, here's
a preamplifier,

01:11:13.280 --> 01:11:14.760
and you'll notice
it's sitting up

01:11:14.760 --> 01:11:21.290
here on top of a padded blanket,
because it's microphonic.

01:11:21.290 --> 01:11:23.175
This preamplifier
is microphonic.

01:11:23.175 --> 01:11:26.762
You have to separate
it from components,

01:11:26.762 --> 01:11:28.820
and you don't want to
bump it, or else you

01:11:28.820 --> 01:11:33.270
will get a false signal
through your circuit.

01:11:33.270 --> 01:11:36.630
Well, with these
sort of improvements,

01:11:36.630 --> 01:11:42.120
this changed things in
a very great fashion.

01:11:42.120 --> 01:11:46.200
One can now put a sample on
here and get things ready

01:11:46.200 --> 01:11:48.480
and go off and do
something else,

01:11:48.480 --> 01:11:52.410
and come back the next
morning or something--

01:11:52.410 --> 01:11:54.840
some later time--
and look at the data,

01:11:54.840 --> 01:11:59.235
or change something and
do things automatically.

01:11:59.235 --> 01:12:03.690
Hey, analysts, spend your time
analyzing things, rather than

01:12:03.690 --> 01:12:05.590
collecting things.

01:12:05.590 --> 01:12:09.930
So I can't overestimate
the importance

01:12:09.930 --> 01:12:12.880
of these
instrumentation features

01:12:12.880 --> 01:12:15.120
that came in at
that time, and they

01:12:15.120 --> 01:12:20.190
were due to our having
these two gentlemen as part

01:12:20.190 --> 01:12:21.840
of the training school--

01:12:21.840 --> 01:12:25.600
students as part of the
training school, there--

01:12:25.600 --> 01:12:29.130
that affected them.

01:12:29.130 --> 01:12:31.260
Well, with this,
then, you could think

01:12:31.260 --> 01:12:39.390
of taking patterns regularly,
and many more over a given time

01:12:39.390 --> 01:12:41.440
span.

01:12:41.440 --> 01:12:45.130
We were always interested
in these cross-sections,

01:12:45.130 --> 01:12:47.950
or amplitudes--

01:12:47.950 --> 01:12:55.400
cross-sections, or amplitudes--
defining just what different

01:12:55.400 --> 01:12:57.680
nuclear species--

01:12:57.680 --> 01:13:00.800
what the real scattering
properties were.

01:13:00.800 --> 01:13:11.610
Because that's data that has
to be obtained empirically,

01:13:11.610 --> 01:13:15.290
and it can't be predicted
from theory-- or at least,

01:13:15.290 --> 01:13:22.520
theory was far behind the
stage where it could predict

01:13:22.520 --> 01:13:24.840
how these things should go.

01:13:24.840 --> 01:13:30.160
So we were very interested
in studying as many samples

01:13:30.160 --> 01:13:35.090
and looking for consistency
from one material to another.

01:13:35.090 --> 01:13:40.610
Does sodium scatter the
same way in sodium chloride

01:13:40.610 --> 01:13:44.610
as in sodium hydroxide?

01:13:44.610 --> 01:13:49.930
That wasn't really
an established thing.

01:13:49.930 --> 01:13:53.160
So we set out to study
these consistencies,

01:13:53.160 --> 01:13:57.900
looking at chlorine
in different species.

01:13:57.900 --> 01:14:04.130
And to our dismay, there was
much that was discrepant.

01:14:04.130 --> 01:14:07.900
And this gave us a
great deal of worry.

01:14:07.900 --> 01:14:12.430
We were trying, at that time,
to look at the total diffraction

01:14:12.430 --> 01:14:14.430
pattern--

01:14:14.430 --> 01:14:16.600
and you still do that.

01:14:16.600 --> 01:14:20.630
You look at the diffuse
scattering, as well as

01:14:20.630 --> 01:14:24.200
the coherence scattering
which is showing up here.

01:14:24.200 --> 01:14:28.010
There should be some-- if
you combine these two things

01:14:28.010 --> 01:14:33.170
together to represent the total
scattering information that

01:14:33.170 --> 01:14:34.640
you're getting--

01:14:34.640 --> 01:14:37.310
there should be consistency.

01:14:37.310 --> 01:14:39.590
We weren't finding that.

01:14:39.590 --> 01:14:46.070
And to illustrate
that, let me show this.

01:14:50.040 --> 01:14:54.380
Here are some observations
made on three different forms

01:14:54.380 --> 01:14:58.670
of pure carbon.

01:14:58.670 --> 01:15:01.310
Diamond, which is pure carbon.

01:15:01.310 --> 01:15:03.290
Graphite-- pure carbon.

01:15:03.290 --> 01:15:07.320
Charcoal-- pure carbon.

01:15:07.320 --> 01:15:13.040
Now, what you'd first think is
that if I put a diamond sample

01:15:13.040 --> 01:15:15.590
on here, graphite
and charcoal, I

01:15:15.590 --> 01:15:17.780
ought to get the
same total scattering

01:15:17.780 --> 01:15:24.015
if I look at everything that
I can see in the scattering

01:15:24.015 --> 01:15:26.250
pattern.

01:15:26.250 --> 01:15:28.820
Turns out that that
isn't the case.

01:15:28.820 --> 01:15:31.950
It seemed not the case to us.

01:15:31.950 --> 01:15:34.890
We were not, when we
added everything up

01:15:34.890 --> 01:15:38.580
in the whole pattern,
including a diffuse scattering

01:15:38.580 --> 01:15:41.730
and coherent
scattering and peaks.

01:15:41.730 --> 01:15:46.070
There was not consistency
from one to another.

01:15:46.070 --> 01:15:51.210
And we looked at other things,
as well as this series.

01:15:51.210 --> 01:15:53.340
And we were having
extreme trouble

01:15:53.340 --> 01:16:02.280
in obtaining what we consider
to be consistent information

01:16:02.280 --> 01:16:04.600
from sample to sample.

01:16:04.600 --> 01:16:06.780
And I remember a
conversation that we had

01:16:06.780 --> 01:16:11.130
with Eugene Wigner at the time.

01:16:11.130 --> 01:16:14.940
Wigner was the Research
Director at the laboratory,

01:16:14.940 --> 01:16:19.110
and Ernie Wollan
and I bent his ear.

01:16:19.110 --> 01:16:24.960
And we were telling him
our tale of woe, here,

01:16:24.960 --> 01:16:29.610
about these inconsistencies that
we were obtaining from sample

01:16:29.610 --> 01:16:31.530
to sample.

01:16:31.530 --> 01:16:34.330
And we were very
puzzled about this,

01:16:34.330 --> 01:16:44.340
and could the great man think
of some help or relief for us?

01:16:44.340 --> 01:16:48.550
Well, he surprised me
very much by saying, no,

01:16:48.550 --> 01:16:53.760
he thought that maybe this
is the way it really is.

01:16:53.760 --> 01:16:59.160
Maybe there's some other
processes, here, going on

01:16:59.160 --> 01:17:01.710
that we just don't understand.

01:17:01.710 --> 01:17:05.800
And he gave us great
encouragement to keep on going,

01:17:05.800 --> 01:17:12.000
and he wasn't taken
aback by the fact

01:17:12.000 --> 01:17:15.630
that we were getting these
seemingly inconsistent

01:17:15.630 --> 01:17:17.220
observations.

01:17:17.220 --> 01:17:20.790
Keep on going-- back
to the laboratory.

01:17:20.790 --> 01:17:24.080
Well, it wasn't
too long after that

01:17:24.080 --> 01:17:28.140
that actually, we
found the solution.

01:17:28.140 --> 01:17:31.260
And the solution
turned out to be this,

01:17:31.260 --> 01:17:36.020
and we were both
embarrassed by knowing it.

01:17:39.610 --> 01:17:44.020
Multiple scattering in samples.

01:17:44.020 --> 01:17:47.650
Multiple scattering in samples.

01:17:47.650 --> 01:17:54.520
Both Ernie and I had had
previous X-ray pattern

01:17:54.520 --> 01:17:59.500
experience where you
scatter X-rays from samples

01:17:59.500 --> 01:18:02.080
and obtain these patterns.

01:18:02.080 --> 01:18:06.305
And we never encountered
a problem of this type.

01:18:06.305 --> 01:18:09.150
But here, things
are very different.

01:18:09.150 --> 01:18:11.340
The neutron penetration
through samples

01:18:11.340 --> 01:18:14.370
is so much larger
than X-ray penetration

01:18:14.370 --> 01:18:20.320
through samples there,
because the cross-sections

01:18:20.320 --> 01:18:21.520
are different.

01:18:24.840 --> 01:18:27.660
In a neutron case
we're using samples

01:18:27.660 --> 01:18:33.180
which are orders of
magnitude thicker, larger

01:18:33.180 --> 01:18:37.440
samples than in the X-ray case.

01:18:37.440 --> 01:18:40.230
And now you're susceptible
to this problem

01:18:40.230 --> 01:18:44.190
of multiple scattering
in the sample block.

01:18:44.190 --> 01:18:47.730
A neutron comes down
to this glob at one

01:18:47.730 --> 01:18:50.910
position, gets scattered
up through the glob--

01:18:50.910 --> 01:18:55.150
partly-- and gets
scattered again.

01:18:55.150 --> 01:18:57.940
And the result is
that you don't have

01:18:57.940 --> 01:19:02.690
consistency in the total
scattering in your pattern.

01:19:02.690 --> 01:19:06.460
So once we recognize that
feature and that fact,

01:19:06.460 --> 01:19:08.760
and we're kicking
ourselves that it took us

01:19:08.760 --> 01:19:11.770
so long to recognize it.

01:19:11.770 --> 01:19:14.180
Then it all became clear to us.

01:19:14.180 --> 01:19:17.710
We had to place
controls on our sample--

01:19:17.710 --> 01:19:21.940
physical, geometrical
conditions--

01:19:21.940 --> 01:19:23.800
which we, of course, did.

01:19:23.800 --> 01:19:27.220
And then things got clarified.

01:19:27.220 --> 01:19:32.680
Now we were able to get
satisfactory consistency

01:19:32.680 --> 01:19:35.230
from one sample
to another sample

01:19:35.230 --> 01:19:40.040
with the recognition of this
troublesome multiple scattering

01:19:40.040 --> 01:19:41.880
problem.

01:19:41.880 --> 01:19:46.520
Well, we had this great
interest in knowing

01:19:46.520 --> 01:19:51.350
whether you could determine
hydrogen atoms in crystals.

01:19:51.350 --> 01:19:54.110
There was this
earlier observation

01:19:54.110 --> 01:19:59.540
that I showed on water, but
that was too complicated

01:19:59.540 --> 01:20:01.110
to interpret.

01:20:01.110 --> 01:20:07.260
So we searched for crystals
that contained hydrogen,

01:20:07.260 --> 01:20:14.780
and which might have a nice,
ordered, crystal structure.

01:20:14.780 --> 01:20:17.720
But it wasn't known whether
the hydrogens were, indeed,

01:20:17.720 --> 01:20:20.450
ordered in these materials.

01:20:20.450 --> 01:20:27.200
Well, one of the simplest of the
hydrogen-containing crystals is

01:20:27.200 --> 01:20:28.430
this--

01:20:28.430 --> 01:20:31.310
sodium hydride.

01:20:31.310 --> 01:20:34.040
Sodium hydride, one-to-one.

01:20:34.040 --> 01:20:36.590
And if you take an
X-ray pattern of this,

01:20:36.590 --> 01:20:38.630
you see only the
sodium and you know

01:20:38.630 --> 01:20:42.950
nothing about where the position
or what the hydrogen atoms are

01:20:42.950 --> 01:20:44.370
doing.

01:20:44.370 --> 01:20:48.260
But the X-ray pattern shows
a well-developed pattern

01:20:48.260 --> 01:20:52.550
characteristic of
a sodium lattice.

01:20:52.550 --> 01:20:56.540
That's known very nicely
from the X-ray pattern.

01:20:56.540 --> 01:21:01.100
But the question is, what
are the hydrogen atoms doing?

01:21:01.100 --> 01:21:04.800
And that's what the
neutrons can hope to show.

01:21:04.800 --> 01:21:07.880
So here are two patterns
of the same position

01:21:07.880 --> 01:21:11.970
in the diffraction
pattern from two

01:21:11.970 --> 01:21:14.340
isotopically different samples--

01:21:14.340 --> 01:21:18.270
sodium deuteride,
and sodium hydride.

01:21:18.270 --> 01:21:22.440
And you see the patterns
are completely different.

01:21:22.440 --> 01:21:24.640
So this says
immediately that you're

01:21:24.640 --> 01:21:30.340
seeing the deuterium or the
hydrogen in these patterns.

01:21:30.340 --> 01:21:33.520
If these were just
looking at sodium atoms,

01:21:33.520 --> 01:21:36.080
they ought to be the same.

01:21:36.080 --> 01:21:37.970
Why the difference?

01:21:37.970 --> 01:21:40.910
Well, the difference
turns out to be

01:21:40.910 --> 01:21:47.660
that the algebraic sign of
the deuterium scattering

01:21:47.660 --> 01:21:53.030
is opposite from the
algebraic sign of the hydrogen

01:21:53.030 --> 01:21:55.090
scattering.

01:21:55.090 --> 01:22:00.190
And that's why the two patterns
are seemingly reversed, here.

01:22:00.190 --> 01:22:02.950
Big peak here, little peak.

01:22:02.950 --> 01:22:04.840
Little peak, big peak.

01:22:04.840 --> 01:22:08.740
And that persists through more
of the diffraction pattern.

01:22:08.740 --> 01:22:12.400
So sure enough, here
what we're seeing

01:22:12.400 --> 01:22:17.880
is the real determination of
the hydrogen atom positions

01:22:17.880 --> 01:22:18.895
in these two crystals.

01:22:21.630 --> 01:22:25.390
This was the first and real
indication that one could,

01:22:25.390 --> 01:22:28.400
and this is a very
important feature.

01:22:28.400 --> 01:22:32.060
Nowadays, there's a lot of
biological structure work that

01:22:32.060 --> 01:22:37.850
goes on with neutron diffraction
in neutron diffraction studies,

01:22:37.850 --> 01:22:41.720
looking at determining where
hydrogen atom positions are

01:22:41.720 --> 01:22:48.860
in organic polymeric,
biological materials.

01:22:48.860 --> 01:22:52.730
That's an important
field of application.

01:22:52.730 --> 01:22:56.390
There are other applications
that I could go on here,

01:22:56.390 --> 01:23:01.530
but I'm afraid I've
gone too long here.

01:23:01.530 --> 01:23:06.110
But let me close by showing
one other pattern, here.

01:23:06.110 --> 01:23:09.320
This is a different
type of neutron pattern.

01:23:09.320 --> 01:23:12.260
This is what's called
a Laue pattern.

01:23:12.260 --> 01:23:16.550
In this Laue pattern, one uses
a direct beam of radiation

01:23:16.550 --> 01:23:18.290
from the reactor--

01:23:18.290 --> 01:23:22.250
not being crystal
monochromated--

01:23:22.250 --> 01:23:26.270
so that you have all
neutron energies,

01:23:26.270 --> 01:23:31.480
all neutron wavelengths
falling on a crystal--

01:23:31.480 --> 01:23:34.220
a single crystal.

01:23:34.220 --> 01:23:37.760
Under such conditions,
the different wavelengths

01:23:37.760 --> 01:23:41.000
and the different atomic
planes in the crystal

01:23:41.000 --> 01:23:46.430
diffract the radiation
to specific directions,

01:23:46.430 --> 01:23:50.120
and one obtains
this spot pattern,

01:23:50.120 --> 01:23:59.230
rather than a peak pattern
as in a powder peak assembly.

01:23:59.230 --> 01:24:04.200
So we have these
Laue spots here.

01:24:04.200 --> 01:24:06.660
Well, this was not the first
picture that was obtained.

01:24:06.660 --> 01:24:09.070
There were quite a
few attempts before

01:24:09.070 --> 01:24:12.220
getting one that looked
even as good as this.

01:24:12.220 --> 01:24:16.370
And this has several
interesting features about it.

01:24:16.370 --> 01:24:22.940
One is that first of
all, it's a photograph.

01:24:22.940 --> 01:24:25.000
There's photographic film.

01:24:25.000 --> 01:24:28.390
And now, photographic film
is insensitive to neutron

01:24:28.390 --> 01:24:29.750
radiation.

01:24:29.750 --> 01:24:32.080
So you use a trick, here.

01:24:32.080 --> 01:24:36.910
You put a piece of scintillating
material next to the film.

01:24:36.910 --> 01:24:41.320
Neutrons come in and are
absorbed in the scintillator.

01:24:41.320 --> 01:24:45.890
In this case, it was a
sheet of indium metal.

01:24:45.890 --> 01:24:50.900
The indium then releases--
after capturing neutrons--

01:24:50.900 --> 01:24:53.600
releases a charged
particle, and that

01:24:53.600 --> 01:24:59.930
charged particle reacts nicely
with the photographic emulsion.

01:24:59.930 --> 01:25:02.990
So the sensitivity comes
from this combination

01:25:02.990 --> 01:25:06.890
of scintillator and
photographic film.

01:25:09.440 --> 01:25:13.400
And there was an indium sheet,
here, covering the film.

01:25:13.400 --> 01:25:15.380
And there was a
section of that cut out

01:25:15.380 --> 01:25:24.110
in a whole circular region
to let the direct beam

01:25:24.110 --> 01:25:26.010
weight it so it
wouldn't scintillate

01:25:26.010 --> 01:25:30.210
and over-cloud the whole film.

01:25:30.210 --> 01:25:32.940
And we've obviously
gotten it off-center.

01:25:32.940 --> 01:25:38.050
The whole assembly is
off-center in the exposure.

01:25:38.050 --> 01:25:40.080
But there are two
other features in here.

01:25:40.080 --> 01:25:43.460
One-- if you look closely
at these spots, you see--

01:25:43.460 --> 01:25:46.410
notice that they all
have a double structure.

01:25:46.410 --> 01:25:48.630
It's particularly
pronounced here.

01:25:52.220 --> 01:25:55.230
There's a double.

01:25:55.230 --> 01:25:58.710
And that puzzles us very much.

01:25:58.710 --> 01:26:03.810
Why should there be this
doubling of neutron spots?

01:26:03.810 --> 01:26:05.940
Is that perhaps
connected with the fact

01:26:05.940 --> 01:26:11.400
that there are two spin states
in the nuclear scattering

01:26:11.400 --> 01:26:14.080
from this crystal?

01:26:14.080 --> 01:26:17.350
Well, we were anxious
to explore that and see

01:26:17.350 --> 01:26:21.700
if we could explain the
origin of this doubling.

01:26:21.700 --> 01:26:26.380
And as it turned out, after
this exposure was made--

01:26:26.380 --> 01:26:33.240
and this exposure took 20
hours, a long-time exposure;

01:26:33.240 --> 01:26:36.100
20-hour exposure--

01:26:36.100 --> 01:26:39.160
just after this
exposure was completed,

01:26:39.160 --> 01:26:46.590
the reactor had to shut down for
breakage of one of these fuel

01:26:46.590 --> 01:26:50.180
element cans.

01:26:50.180 --> 01:26:53.320
One of the cans
containing the uranium

01:26:53.320 --> 01:26:57.460
had developed a pinhole,
and the uranium inside it

01:26:57.460 --> 01:27:04.100
oxidized, and it had essentially
exploded inside the reactor.

01:27:04.100 --> 01:27:06.680
So there was no way of
operating the reactor

01:27:06.680 --> 01:27:07.880
under those conditions.

01:27:07.880 --> 01:27:09.900
They had to get it out of there.

01:27:09.900 --> 01:27:13.810
And that took some three
weeks for them to cleanse that

01:27:13.810 --> 01:27:16.070
and get back to operation.

01:27:16.070 --> 01:27:19.880
So it took us a long time to
ponder over this before we

01:27:19.880 --> 01:27:24.290
could hope to try to do
something else to try

01:27:24.290 --> 01:27:30.140
to explain this doubling
of these Laue spots.

01:27:30.140 --> 01:27:33.740
Well, it turned out, after
we got back in business,

01:27:33.740 --> 01:27:35.720
that the spot
structure was something

01:27:35.720 --> 01:27:39.320
associated with a
particular crystal we used.

01:27:39.320 --> 01:27:44.600
The crystal had a mosaic
layer on its two faces--

01:27:44.600 --> 01:27:46.610
entrance and exit.

01:27:46.610 --> 01:27:49.840
And a mosaic layer
means that the crystal

01:27:49.840 --> 01:27:53.070
scatters more strongly.

01:27:53.070 --> 01:27:58.340
So what these spots
represent are, effectively,

01:27:58.340 --> 01:28:00.620
scattering from the
front side of the crystal

01:28:00.620 --> 01:28:02.660
and the back side
of the crystal.

01:28:02.660 --> 01:28:05.960
And that's, somehow or other,
showing up all around here.

01:28:05.960 --> 01:28:08.360
So that was a
trivial explanation.

01:28:08.360 --> 01:28:13.680
A good crystal without this
mosaic layer on the surface

01:28:13.680 --> 01:28:15.530
doesn't show this.

01:28:15.530 --> 01:28:17.470
But another
interesting feature--

01:28:17.470 --> 01:28:20.130
you notice that there's
a band structure, here.

01:28:24.140 --> 01:28:32.000
Well, there's no way
you would expect that.

01:28:32.000 --> 01:28:38.280
That turns out to be the world's
first neutron radium graph

01:28:38.280 --> 01:28:41.100
feature.

01:28:41.100 --> 01:28:45.780
The band structure
represents the collection

01:28:45.780 --> 01:28:48.270
between pieces of indium.

01:28:48.270 --> 01:28:51.570
We didn't have enough indium,
or in large enough area

01:28:51.570 --> 01:28:56.880
to cover this thing, so we had
strips that we kept together

01:28:56.880 --> 01:29:01.050
by strips of scotch tape.

01:29:01.050 --> 01:29:02.880
Scotch tape line.

01:29:02.880 --> 01:29:04.080
Scotch tape line.

01:29:04.080 --> 01:29:05.010
Another one here.

01:29:05.010 --> 01:29:05.590
[LAUGHTER]

01:29:05.590 --> 01:29:08.970
So this is showing the
radiographing of the Scotch

01:29:08.970 --> 01:29:12.840
tape that was used to hold
these indium strips together.

01:29:12.840 --> 01:29:15.350
Well, that set us
off to thinking,

01:29:15.350 --> 01:29:16.830
what else could we do?

01:29:16.830 --> 01:29:20.610
If a piece of Scotch tape
shows up here like this,

01:29:20.610 --> 01:29:27.840
there must be better uses
that one can exploit in this.

01:29:27.840 --> 01:29:29.580
Indeed, that's the case.

01:29:29.580 --> 01:29:35.400
People now radiograph large
assemblies, metal castings,

01:29:35.400 --> 01:29:39.540
or other things with
neutrons, in the way

01:29:39.540 --> 01:29:43.470
that normal
radiographing is done,

01:29:43.470 --> 01:29:48.160
and exploiting the particular
penetration properties

01:29:48.160 --> 01:29:53.230
of the neutron radiation.

01:29:53.230 --> 01:29:57.330
Well, these were very
exciting times back then,

01:29:57.330 --> 01:30:04.290
and I often think really
how exciting it was.

01:30:04.290 --> 01:30:07.530
Almost any corner
you turned, there'd

01:30:07.530 --> 01:30:10.860
be new vistas showing up.

01:30:10.860 --> 01:30:16.790
And we explored many,
many corners at the time.

01:30:16.790 --> 01:30:23.280
But on reflection, I'm sure
that there's no difference

01:30:23.280 --> 01:30:25.860
today than back then.

01:30:25.860 --> 01:30:30.240
Almost anything that
one can look at,

01:30:30.240 --> 01:30:34.800
there is new information
and new things to uncover.

01:30:34.800 --> 01:30:41.250
So I think the challenges
in present-day physics,

01:30:41.250 --> 01:30:46.830
present-day science
loom just as important,

01:30:46.830 --> 01:30:51.370
as prominently as they
did back at this time.

01:30:51.370 --> 01:30:57.300
So I say that to our younger
friends who are with us here.

01:30:57.300 --> 01:30:59.070
Thank you very much.

01:30:59.070 --> 01:31:00.320
PRESENTER: Neutron scattering.

01:31:00.320 --> 01:31:01.676
Cliff's stories are rich.

01:31:01.676 --> 01:31:03.050
He actually only
began to scratch

01:31:03.050 --> 01:31:05.220
the surface of all his
many contributions--

01:31:05.220 --> 01:31:08.730
magnetism, polarized neutron
technology, basic neutron

01:31:08.730 --> 01:31:09.480
properties.

01:31:09.480 --> 01:31:13.578
I guess remain for the
next installment of that.

01:31:13.578 --> 01:31:14.450
[LAUGHTER]

01:31:14.450 --> 01:31:16.314
Cliff has agreed to
answer some questions,

01:31:16.314 --> 01:31:20.286
so we have time for
perhaps a few, at least.

01:31:20.286 --> 01:31:23.557
Questions or comments?

01:31:23.557 --> 01:31:24.057
Bob?

01:31:24.057 --> 01:31:26.939
BIRGENEAU: Cliff, when and
how did you first get the idea

01:31:26.939 --> 01:31:28.752
that you could look at
antiparallel magents

01:31:28.752 --> 01:31:31.430
with this technique?

01:31:31.430 --> 01:31:34.900
SHULL: That came a year
or two after these things

01:31:34.900 --> 01:31:38.030
that I've chatted about.

01:31:38.030 --> 01:31:44.600
And we were set
off on this route

01:31:44.600 --> 01:31:47.450
by our desire to
look for what was

01:31:47.450 --> 01:31:50.990
called paramagnetic scattering.

01:31:50.990 --> 01:31:52.910
Paramagnetic scattering
was something

01:31:52.910 --> 01:31:59.990
that theorists had predicted
would occur from a material--

01:31:59.990 --> 01:32:02.550
a paramagnetic material.

01:32:02.550 --> 01:32:07.250
And at that time, a paramagnetic
material-- as it is today--

01:32:07.250 --> 01:32:11.600
represent a collection of
magnetic centers, where there

01:32:11.600 --> 01:32:15.440
was randomness in the
direction or the orientation

01:32:15.440 --> 01:32:17.720
of these magnetic centers--

01:32:17.720 --> 01:32:20.090
paramagnetic material.

01:32:20.090 --> 01:32:23.330
In contrast to a
ferromagnetic material,

01:32:23.330 --> 01:32:27.490
where they were considered
to be all lined up.

01:32:27.490 --> 01:32:32.500
Now, in paramagnetics,
with a paramagnetic sample,

01:32:32.500 --> 01:32:35.160
if one scatters
neutrons from it,

01:32:35.160 --> 01:32:38.830
you expect to find
a diffuse scattering

01:32:38.830 --> 01:32:43.670
in the pattern associated
with this random collection

01:32:43.670 --> 01:32:46.640
of magnetic centers.

01:32:46.640 --> 01:32:52.220
This was shown in a very
powerful theoretical paper

01:32:52.220 --> 01:32:56.570
by Halpern and Johnson back in--

01:32:56.570 --> 01:33:00.350
years earlier than this
period I was talking about.

01:33:03.740 --> 01:33:06.650
And so this was a
prediction, and one

01:33:06.650 --> 01:33:10.290
that was capable of observation
with the technology that

01:33:10.290 --> 01:33:12.710
was available here.

01:33:12.710 --> 01:33:14.810
So we were interested
in looking for

01:33:14.810 --> 01:33:20.100
this paramagnetic scattering
in a paramagnetic media.

01:33:20.100 --> 01:33:24.140
And the first thing that we
looked at was in a material--

01:33:24.140 --> 01:33:27.200
a typical paramagnetic
substance--

01:33:27.200 --> 01:33:30.570
hematite.

01:33:30.570 --> 01:33:35.110
Fe2O3-- hematite.

01:33:35.110 --> 01:33:37.940
There are strong magnetic ions--

01:33:37.940 --> 01:33:42.620
iron ions-- in this substance.

01:33:42.620 --> 01:33:46.230
And it was known that you
could make or you could measure

01:33:46.230 --> 01:33:52.760
paramagnetic susceptibility
and obtain certain values

01:33:52.760 --> 01:33:55.880
of the susceptibility,
and could interpret that--

01:33:55.880 --> 01:33:58.430
its temperature dependence--

01:33:58.430 --> 01:34:01.580
upon the strength of
these magnetic ions,

01:34:01.580 --> 01:34:03.570
which were at random.

01:34:03.570 --> 01:34:06.440
So we looked at that first.

01:34:06.440 --> 01:34:10.790
And that was present.

01:34:10.790 --> 01:34:13.880
We could see that.

01:34:13.880 --> 01:34:15.830
We also looked at another one--

01:34:15.830 --> 01:34:17.960
manganous oxide--
where, again, you

01:34:17.960 --> 01:34:22.340
have a strongly-magnetic
manganese ion

01:34:22.340 --> 01:34:24.320
present in the material.

01:34:24.320 --> 01:34:31.010
Manganous oxide-- monovalent,
strong, magnetic ion.

01:34:31.010 --> 01:34:35.960
And that, again, has a nice
paramagnetic susceptibility.

01:34:35.960 --> 01:34:41.030
And we looked at that, and
what we found there was there

01:34:41.030 --> 01:34:44.010
was, indeed, this
diffuse scattering,

01:34:44.010 --> 01:34:47.510
but it showed some structure
in the diffuse scattering.

01:34:47.510 --> 01:34:51.540
Instead of showing
a uniformly varying

01:34:51.540 --> 01:34:56.440
paramagnetic scattering,
it showed some structure.

01:34:56.440 --> 01:34:58.460
And that suggested--

01:34:58.460 --> 01:35:03.100
I had had experience with
short and long-range order

01:35:03.100 --> 01:35:06.260
in crystals prior to that time.

01:35:06.260 --> 01:35:10.280
That suggested that if we were
to cool this medium down--

01:35:10.280 --> 01:35:17.780
the sample down-- that this
short-range order might develop

01:35:17.780 --> 01:35:21.980
into coherent long-range order.

01:35:21.980 --> 01:35:26.150
And sure enough, when we
finally did that experiment,

01:35:26.150 --> 01:35:31.330
by cooling the manganous oxide
down to a low temperature,

01:35:31.330 --> 01:35:33.910
the long-range
order had developed.

01:35:33.910 --> 01:35:36.500
So the origin of the
magnetic scattering

01:35:36.500 --> 01:35:41.360
all came in the
interest of searching

01:35:41.360 --> 01:35:44.120
for the paramagnetic
effects, and then

01:35:44.120 --> 01:35:48.920
the ordered magnetic lattice
scattering followed from that.

01:35:48.920 --> 01:35:54.770
But that came in
1948, '49, 1950--

01:35:54.770 --> 01:35:57.530
a couple years after
these experiments

01:35:57.530 --> 01:36:02.302
that I've just been describing.

01:36:02.302 --> 01:36:03.799
PRESENTER: Other questions?

01:36:03.799 --> 01:36:06.294
Looks like we're coming
to a certain symmetry

01:36:06.294 --> 01:36:08.300
since you started
with the Clinton pile.

01:36:08.300 --> 01:36:11.020
And of course, now one hopes
to build the new reactor

01:36:11.020 --> 01:36:12.395
in Oak Ridge, and
if it succeeds,

01:36:12.395 --> 01:36:14.162
it will certainly be the Gore--

01:36:14.162 --> 01:36:14.870
the Gore reactor.

01:36:14.870 --> 01:36:17.786
So it will be the Clinton-Gore
reactor in history.

01:36:17.786 --> 01:36:19.160
[LAUGHTER]

01:36:19.160 --> 01:36:20.770
The only difference
is the 11 months

01:36:20.770 --> 01:36:22.230
it took for the Clinton reactor.

01:36:22.230 --> 01:36:24.896
It'll be 11 years at least
for the Gore reactor.

01:36:24.896 --> 01:36:27.362
With that, let's thank
Cliff once again.

01:36:27.362 --> 01:36:28.070
It was wonderful.

01:36:28.070 --> 01:36:31.420
[APPLAUSE]

